/* ============ COFFEE & TEA ============
   Nine sections, shaped exactly like ONTAP_STUDY and SERVICE_STUDY so one
   renderer pattern, one search-index pattern and one check in tools/check.mjs
   cover all three.

   This tab is REFERENCE AND VIDEO, not a drill deck. COFFEE_REF is deliberately
   absent from DECK_SOURCES, allDrinks, groupsFor and cardTicket, which is the
   older Behind the Stick shape rather than the newer On Tap one. SERVICE_REF
   has shipped that way since the beginning and nothing in either tree assumes
   otherwise: every consumer of a *_REF array names its array explicitly, and
   there is no generic loop over reference tabs. If it ever should become a
   deck source, the shape is already right and the change is a handful of lines
   in ui-study.js.

   The drinks stay where they live. This app already holds 22 coffee and tea
   cocktails, 21 zero-proof drinks under a category called Coffee, Tea & Warm,
   and 12 shots, each with its own lore. Spiked & Service indexes them and
   links out to them; it does not copy a single spec, because a cocktail name
   is a spaced repetition primary key and moving one rewrites study records.

   EVERY WORD IN THIS FILE IS WRITTEN WITHOUT AN EM DASH, on purpose. The
   monorepo counts them across the published tree against a baseline of 14 with
   nine already spent, so dash-free prose is what lets this file be byte
   identical in both trees rather than two diverging copies.
   ============ */

const COFFEE_STUDY = [
  { key:"machine", title:"The Machine", rows:[
    ["What the machine actually does",
     "Espresso is not strong coffee, which is the belief to kill first. Drip drains at atmospheric pressure and a press just steeps, but an espresso machine forces water near 200F through a compacted bed of very fine grounds, and the pressure changes what comes out. You dose a modern double basket at about 18 to 20 grams, tamp it level, lock the portafilter into the group, and run roughly twice the dose in liquid, 36 to 40 grams, in 25 to 30 seconds. Dose in against liquid out is the brew ratio, which with grind is most of your control. Nine bar, about 130 psi, is a benchmark rather than a constant: preinfusion runs below it and profiling machines move it through the shot. Tamping is argued about; level matters more than force, and the thirty pound rule has few defenders left. It carries roughly 8 to 12 percent dissolved solids against filter coffee's 1.15 to 1.35, plus emulsified oil and fine solids a paper filter strips out. Crema is roast carbon dioxide, driven into solution under pressure and foaming out as the pressure drops, so it reads freshness and blend rather than quality. Espresso is a method, not a bean or a roast level, and any coffee can run through it."],
    ["Boilers, and what the machine can do at once",
     "The only boiler question a bar has is what the machine does while ten drinks land at once, and the line that matters is whether it brews and steams together. A single boiler dual use machine cannot: you brew near 200F, flip to steam, wait for the boiler to climb, steam, then wait longer for it to fall back, and that wait is the minute you do not have. A thermoblock heats water in a metal block as it passes, storing almost nothing, so it switches quickly but steams thinly and stalls on the second milk drink. A heat exchanger holds one boiler at steam pressure, usually one to one and a half bar or about 250 to 260F, with a tube of fresh water running through it that reaches brew temperature on the way to the group, so one boiler does both, as most bar machines do. The cost is that water standing in that tube overheats while the machine idles, so flush the group before the first shot and after any long pause. A dual boiler holds each separately, buying steady brew temperature and no flush rather than extra speed. The wrong belief is that the second boiler is what saves you in a rush. What saves you is never having to choose."],
    ["Temperature is the variable nobody watches",
     "Water at the group runs between 190 and 205F, and the trade does not agree inside it: Italian certification sits low, specialty houses high, and set points move with roast, lower for dark, higher for light. The wrong belief is that the machine simply holds it. A boiler holds its set point, which is not the temperature of the water meeting your coffee. Many machines run a pressurestat, a switch that lets pressure and so temperature swing through a cycle, as a small machine's thermostat does too, so managing it by hand is not one drill: on a heat exchanger you flush the group until the standing water drops back into range, on a single boiler you surf the heating light, starting every shot at the same point in the cycle. A PID holds the point it measures to about a degree, not the puck. The group is the other half, a heavy lump of brass that has to be hot, so give it half an hour from cold, longer on a heavy one, and leave a portafilter locked in. A cold group reads sour, thin and hollow; a hot one bitter, ashy and dry. Same dose, same grind, same time, different cup: check the group before you blame the grinder."],
    ["The grinder sets what the machine cannot fix",
     "The wrong belief is that the machine is the decisive purchase and the grinder an accessory, so a better machine will rescue bad shots. Nothing downstream undoes particle size. A blade grinder chops, making dust and boulders in the same breath, and that bed cannot extract evenly: boulders give up too little because water only reaches their outsides, while dust gives up too much and packs tight enough to choke the flow, so you taste under-extraction and over-extraction in one mouthful. Burrs fracture coffee between two surfaces a set gap apart, so size becomes a setting, not an accident, and dull burrs widen the spread. Flats sit face to face, conicals turn a cone inside a ring, and the trade reads flats as clarity and conicals as body; offer that as house taste, not measured fact, since alignment, sharpness and speed move a cup more than shape does. Channelling gets pinned on grind alone, but puck prep and distribution are argued over as hard. The machine still owns temperature stability and steam. Behind a bar, retention bites hardest: grounds stranded in the chute and burr chamber mean part of your dose is the last drink's coffee, sitting in air. Purge and bin a short dose whenever the grinder has stood idle."],
    ["Water, and the scale that kills machines",
     "Two things in your water matter and most bars run them together. Hardness is calcium and magnesium. Alkalinity is bicarbonate and carbonate, the buffer that neutralises acid. Get the mechanism right: scale is not hardness alone, it is hardness and alkalinity together, because heat drives the bicarbonate over and calcium carbonate falls out on the hottest surfaces, the element, the boiler wall, the heat exchanger tube, where it insulates the element until it overheats and fails, chokes tubing and seizes valves. Hard water low in alkalinity scales far less than its hardness number suggests, so one reading tells you nothing. Alkalinity also decides the cup: too much and a bright coffee reads dull and chalky, too little and the acidity comes through thin and sour. The wrong belief is that the filter on your line is scale protection. Plain carbon takes out chlorine and off tastes and touches neither hardness nor alkalinity; only softening, dealkalising or sequestering media do, so learn what is in your cartridge. Pure is not the safe end either: water stripped bare tastes hollow, corrodes metal because nothing buffers it, and where a boiler senses level by conductivity it can read as empty and let the fill valve flood it. Treat, remineralise, then test."],
    ["Backflushing and the cleaning you cannot skip",
     "The wrong belief is that hot water through that group all day keeps it clean. Every shot leaves coffee oil on the shower screen, the dispersion block and the three-way valve, and oil is a fat, so water alone does not carry it off. It oxidises and turns rancid, bitter as old fryer oil, and no fresh coffee covers that. Backflushing locks a blind basket, one with no holes, into the group. Water cannot pass down, so the pump builds pressure in the group; the flush comes when you stop it and the valve dumps that pressure to the drain, driving the water back up through the screen and the oil out with it. So run short bursts, not one long pull. At close, brush the screen and gasket, backflush with a detergent made for espresso machines, never dish soap, rinse the blind basket, repeat with water until it runs clear, then bin the first shot. Manuals differ on how often the detergent goes in, so follow yours. Weekly, soak baskets and portafilter bodies in that detergent, handles out. A hardened gasket lets the handle sit further round than it did and water squirt from the rim mid-shot; replace it. One hard warning: never blind backflush a machine with no three-way valve."],
    ["The grinder needs cleaning too, and differently",
     "The grinder looks clean because coffee is all that goes through it, and that is the wrong belief. Bean oil films the hopper and the chute, goes rancid as it does on a shower screen, and taints every dose that passes. Keep water out of the grinding path: steel burrs rust, and grounds plus water set like cement in the throat. Everything below the hopper is cleaned dry. The hopper gets a dry wipe weekly at least, and goes to the sink only if the manual allows and it comes back bone dry. Deeper, at an interval your volume sets: unplug it, empty the hopper, run the burrs empty, lift out the upper burr, brush burrs, chamber and chute, and vacuum rather than blow, which drives fines into the bearings. Cleaning tablets stretch that interval rather than replacing the strip down, and you purge coffee after them until it runs clean. Rice is not a grinder cleaner. Then the step everybody skips: a burr that comes off will not seat exactly as before, so the setting is gone. Dial back in with scale and timer before service, tighten only while the burrs turn, not into a static bed of grounds, and purge after every adjustment because the chute holds the old grind."],
    ["Reading a machine you have never used",
     "The wrong belief is that an espresso machine is a standard appliance, one button doing the same thing everywhere. Read it before a guest is waiting. (1) Gauges: one needle is the steam boiler, which should sit inside its printed band, commonly 1 to 1.5 bar, and a needle at zero means no steam yet. The other is brew pressure, and it only reads high against a packed basket. Nine bar is the traditional target, but many machines run lower or let pressure fall through the shot, and a lever runs off a spring, not a pump, and often has no brew gauge, so learn that machine's normal. (2) Heat: is a portafilter parked in the group, and how long has it been on, because ten minutes is not enough. (3) Basket: pull it out and look. Single, double, or a dual wall pressurised basket that makes foam through one small hole and barely answers grind. (4) Grind: find the collar, note where it sits, change nothing without asking. (5) Ask the house dose and recipe, then pull one to a cup, weighing in and out and timing from the pump. Roughly double the dry weight in 25 to 30 seconds, tasting sweet, is in business. Gushing means too coarse, choking means too fine."],
  ] },
  { key:"shot", title:"The Shot", rows:[
    ["Dose, yield and ratio",
     "A shot is not a volume. It is two weights and the ratio they make. Dose is the dry coffee in the basket, weighed in grams. Yield is the liquid espresso in the cup, also weighed in grams on a scale, not read off a line in a shot glass. Ratio sets the two against each other with the dose first, so 18 grams in and 36 grams out is 1:2. You weigh the yield because crema is foam and foam is mostly gas, so two shots with identical liquid can sit at very different heights in the glass, and the crema falls while you walk the drink out. The ranges you will meet: a double basket takes 18 to 20 grams, a single 7 to 10, and a modern normale about 1:2, with houses running 1:1.5 to 1:2.5. Traditional Italian practice is a smaller dose, near 7 grams, and it is specified as a volume in the cup including crema rather than a weight, which works out longer against the dose than 1:2, not tighter. An ounce of espresso in a cocktail spec is a cocktail measure, not a brewing target. Hand over dose and yield together at shift change, because a double tells the next person nothing at all."],
    ["Time is a symptom, not a target",
     "Your machine has a timer on it, and that is the trap. A double at about 1:2 is commonly taught to land in twenty-five to thirty seconds, so the number is worth knowing, but it is a symptom, not a target. You do not set time. Time is what the bed does when water is pushed through it, and it moves with the grind, the dose, the basket, the water temperature and the pump behind them. Start the clock at the pump and stop it when the scale hits your yield, and make the bar do it the same way, because a house that starts at first drip reads shorter on the same shot, and the two numbers cannot be compared. The mistake is chasing the clock with the wrong lever: packing in more coffee, which does slow it but changes the drink you sold; leaning harder on the tamp, where the trade still argues about how much force matters once the bed is level; or cutting dead on thirty seconds whatever is in the cup. A slow shot cut at thirty seconds is short of its yield, and short is under-extracted however long it took. Hold dose and basket, move the grind. If it tastes right at thirty-four seconds, serve it."],
    ["Grind is the only dial you turn",
     "Hold the dose, the ratio and the water temperature, and move the grind. Finer means more surface area and a tighter bed, so water meets more resistance, runs slower, and takes more out. Coarser means less resistance, faster flow, less out. Fast and sour, go finer. Slow and harsh, go coarser. The span between watery and choked is narrower than the knob looks, so move in the smallest increment your grinder offers and taste before moving again. Change one thing at a time and say out loud what you changed, because a shot moved in three places teaches you nothing about any of them. Old grind sits in the burrs and the chute after every adjustment, so purge or pull a throwaway before you judge the change, or you are tasting two grinds at once. The wrong belief is that tamp, dose and time are dials too. Time is the readout you move the grind to change, not something you set. Dose is fixed by the basket, and moving it moves the ratio too. Tamp is the one the trade still argues about, some houses drilling a fixed pressure and others holding that past a level, compacted bed more force changes little, so tamp the same way every time instead of reaching for it."],
    ["Distribution and tamping",
     "Water goes wherever it meets the least resistance, so your job is to make every part of the puck equally hard to get through. That is a distribution problem first, a tamping problem second. Grinders throw clumps, so break them up and level the bed before you tamp; stirring the dry grounds with fine needles, the tool the trade calls a WDT, does more for a shot than any amount of arm. Then tamp, and here is what people have backwards. Once the bed is consolidated it barely compresses further, and the pump pushes far harder than your arm, so firm and heroic end up much the same. Level against tilted is the gap that matters: tilt the tamper and one side of the bed is left looser, and water takes that side, not the one you pressed. The trade has repeated thirty pounds of force for decades; read that as a description of firm, not a number you could hit without a scale under the tamper. Press straight down, the same way every time, and stop. Harder does not make it stronger, and your wrist pays for it. The trade still argues how much of channelling is tamp rather than grind, dose or basket, so make yours the one thing that never varies."],
    ["Channelling, and how to see it",
     "Channelling is water finding a crack and taking it. Instead of pushing evenly through the bed it carves a route, over-extracting along it while the coffee either side is barely touched, which is why the shot tastes sour and harsh at once and finishes thin. Usually it runs fast, since the channel is the path of least resistance, blonding early and hitting yield early, and grinding finer to slow it often channels the next one. The trap is the quiet case: a shot that lands in a normal window, eighteen grams in to thirty six out in twenty five to thirty seconds, and still tastes broken. Time and weight say a shot was normal, not that it was even. A bottomless portafilter, also called naked, has no spouts, so the basket underside shows. Healthy looks like drips across the whole surface drawing into one dark steady stream, the mouse tail. Channelling sprays sideways, jets from one spot, or goes pale on one side while the rest stays dark. Check the bed first: (1) clumpy distribution, (2) a tilted tamp, (3) a knock after tamping, (4) grounds on the rim breaking the seal, (5) a dose the basket cannot take, high or low. Grind can contribute, but fix the bed before you chase it."],
    ["Sour and bitter are the two directions of extraction",
     "Coffee gives up its solubles in a rough order: acids and salts early, then the compounds that read as sweet, then the bitter, drying ones last. So extraction fails in two directions. Under-extracted, you stopped too early in that order: sour and sharp, salty at the edges, thin, and it quits the second you swallow. Over-extracted, you ran past the sweet middle: bitter, ashy, hollow, drying on the tongue, with a finish that will not leave. The 18 to 22 percent extraction target you will hear quoted came from filter brewing, and whether it fits espresso is argued, so do not repeat it as a rule. Learn both faults before service, one shot ground far too coarse and one far too fine, because you cannot recognise a fault you have never tasted. Extraction is not strength: a well extracted shot can still be too concentrated for the guest, and that is ratio, not grind. Extraction is also not the only fault, because a dark roast, stale beans, a dirty machine or scorched milk all arrive bitter with the grind innocent. Guests say bitter for anything unpleasant, so when a coffee comes back do not pull it weaker or bury it in milk on the word alone. Taste it yourself and name the fault."],
    ["Dialling in, as a procedure",
     "Here is the loop for a new bag at open. (1) Weigh a dose your basket is built for and keep it there, because you change one thing at a time. (2) Pick the target yield from the ratio, 1:2 to start, so 18 grams in and 36 out. (3) Guess the grind from where the last bag lived, coarser if this one is fresh and still gassing, finer if it is roasted lighter, because a harder bean grinds coarser. (4) Purge a few grams so you are not grinding the last bag. (5) Distribute, tamp level, lock in and pull straight onto a scale, stopping at the target yield and noting the time. (6) Taste it, then taste it in the milk drink your bar sells, because a thin shot vanishes under steamed milk. (7) Sour and fast, one step finer. Harsh and slow, one step coarser. If it sprays or gushes, suspect the puck before the grinder and repull first. (8) Purge and run the loop again. Stop when two in a row taste good and taste alike, not when the clock reads a number you like. Expect to bin several shots, then dial again when the room changes and on the next bag."],
    ["Ristretto, normale, lungo",
     "Same dose, same puck, different yield. Ristretto means restricted, roughly 1:1 to 1:1.5, so 18 grams into a double basket and 18 to 27 grams out, weighed not eyeballed. Normale is about 1:2. Lungo means long, roughly 1:3 to 1:4, so that same 18 grams pushed out to 54 or 72 grams. Now the wrong belief: a lungo is not an espresso with water added. That drink is an americano, and diluting after the fact lowers concentration without changing how much came out of the grounds. A lungo is more water forced through the same coffee, and the water arriving last carries the bitter, drying end, so it is less concentrated and more extracted at once, which is why it often reads harsh and thin rather than gentle. Most houses grind coarser for it. A ristretto runs the other way: you stop before that drying end arrives, so it is less extracted but more concentrated, which is the intensity a guest means by stronger, though less water through the puck means slightly less total caffeine. Send the guest who wants it milder to the americano, never to the lungo. The trade does not fully agree on the words, and some bars still call a watered shot a lungo, so confirm what your house means."],
    ["Freshness, degassing and the bag date",
     "The wrong belief is that fresher is always better and that the date on the bag settles it. Roasting loads the bean with carbon dioxide, and it takes days to leave. Pull coffee too soon off the roast and the gas fights you: the puck sputters and channels, the same grind setting will not repeat, and the cup reads sharp and thin because gas keeps water off the coffee. Thick crema here is gas and roast level, not quality. So coffee wants a rest, commonly 7 to 14 days off roast for espresso and longer than filter asks for, though dark roasts are often ready sooner and light ones want longer. It then holds for weeks. Once the bag is open the clock speeds up, because the enemy is oxygen rather than gas, so keep it whole bean, sealed, cool, dark, and out of the fridge, which is damp and full of smells. The one-way valve lets gas out without letting air in, so it tells you the bag is built for coffee that still gasses, not that this one is fresh. A best-by date is the roast date plus an offset the roaster chose, so ask for the offset and write the real date on the bag yourself."],
    ["Decaf is not an afterthought",
     "Decaf gets pulled last, on your regular settings, by somebody who has stopped caring, and it is the one order most likely to have a medical reason sitting behind it. Start with what happened to the bean. Every method begins by wetting green coffee, by soaking or steaming, and then carries the caffeine off with a solvent such as methylene chloride or ethyl acetate, with pressurised carbon dioxide, or with water and green coffee extract, as the Swiss Water and Mountain Water processes do. All of it swells the bean and leaves it more brittle and more porous. Brittle means it shatters into more fines at the same setting, which is why the usual starting move on espresso is coarser, but enough bars find the opposite that you dial decaf in on its own and let the shot decide. Porous means it stales faster, so buy smaller bags more often. It also roasts darker, so colour will lie to you about the roast level. If one grinder does both, grind a dose and bin it before you grind theirs, or the first shot is not decaf. And it is not caffeine free: the standard commonly quoted is at least 97 percent of the caffeine removed, so tell a guest nearly none and never zero."],
  ] },
  { key:"milk", title:"Milk & Latte Art", rows:[
    ["What steaming actually does to milk",
     "The wrong belief is that steaming is heating and that foam is a side effect of the heat. Steam does two jobs and you steer them separately. The first is stretching, also called aeration: the jet drags air through the surface and shears it into bubbles, and that stage ends early, while the milk is still cool. The second is texturing, or rolling: the same jet spins the milk hard enough to break those bubbles smaller and fold them under, which is what gets you microfoam instead of froth. Heat is not doing either job; it is your clock. What holds a bubble open is protein: the whey proteins unfold on the surface of each bubble and knit into a film, and casein helps hold it. Fat lends the body you read as creamy but builds no structure, and it costs volume, which is why skim foams bigger and drier. As for sweetness, you make no sugar and, with no lactase in the pitcher, break none down, so the roughly five percent lactose is what it always was. Warmth is credited with lifting perceived sweetness, but that is not settled, so do not sell it as chemistry. Take it too far and those proteins turn eggy, and cooked is not sweet."],
    ["The temperature ceiling",
     "The wrong belief is that hotter is better service, that steaming splits lactose into something sweeter, and that a scalded pitcher can be rescued by re-steaming or by a fresh shot over it. Work a band: cut the steam near 135F to 140F, let carryover walk it up a few degrees, and serve between 140F and 155F. That band is where the milk reads sweetest, where the foam still pours a pattern, and where the cup is still hot at the last sip. Reads is the honest word: steaming makes no new sugar and breaks no lactose down, so only your perception moves. Past roughly 160F the whey protein that was holding your bubbles finishes unfolding and cross-links to itself: the bubbles coarsen and the foam separates, and sulfur compounds come off as the boiled, faintly eggy note nobody orders. Blame heat for that, not for stiff dry foam, which is usually too much air. Here is the part people refuse. It does not come back. Cross-linked protein does not re-fold, and re-steaming only spends more heat on milk that already spent it. For extra hot, say plainly it will taste flatter, take it to that same ceiling and no further, in a handled cup. Dump anything you cooked."],
    ["Stretch, then spin",
     "The wrong belief is that you find one right spot and hold it. There is an air phase, a spin phase, and a move between them. (1) Purge the wand, then set the tip just under the surface and off center, so the jet drives a whirlpool around the wall. (2) Open the valve in one motion rather than feathering it, because jet force is what turns that whirlpool. Wide open suits a commercial boiler; low pressure wands and small volumes are often easier part open, so let the house machine decide. (3) Stretch. You want a steady tearing sound, a rhythmic tss tss, each chirp one bite of air. A shriek means the tip has broken the surface and is making big dry bubbles, not microfoam. The milk rises as it takes air, so the pitcher comes down to keep that depth. (4) Stop the air early, while the pitcher still reads cool against your palm, which holds near 100F. Air added later has no spin time left to break it down. (5) Sink the tip deeper until the hiss is gone and only a low rumble remains. That is texturing, folding the foam back through the milk, and it runs until the pitcher is too hot to hold."],
    ["The vortex, and what it is for",
     "The wrong belief is that the whirlpool is showmanship. It is the mechanism: bubbles get smaller while the steam runs or not at all, because tapping and swirling afterwards only pop the worst of what is left. The air you added in the stretch went in at mixed sizes; the spin drives the surface down and folds it under, and the shear at the jet tears the big ones apart and keeps tearing them for the rest of the steam. It also mixes the jug, because milk that is not moving stratifies: scalded where the jet sits, cool at the base, dry foam on top. To find it, put the tip off the center axis, between the middle and the wall and touching neither, and go deep enough that the surface dimples and turns rather than sputters. Some trainers tilt the pitcher for it and some keep it flat; your wand angle and jug settle that, so copy your own bar. You should see the whole body of milk turning. Without it you get a stiff cap of foam on thin milk that will not pour: the foam hangs back at the spout, then arrives all at once as a white blob on a beige cup, and no wrist saves it."],
    ["Pitcher, volume and the pour window",
     "The wrong belief is that steaming extra is thrift. Size the pitcher to the drink, not to the shift. The common pair is a twelve ounce and a twenty ounce, and the working fill is just below where the spout begins, because milk gains volume. That is a ceiling, not a target: too little in a big pitcher and the milk is too shallow to keep the tip submerged and spinning a vortex, and it overheats before you texture it. Go over and you wear it. The number on the pitcher is not the milk you pour, so measure yours to the spout once: the twelve covers one small drink, the twenty a large one or two at once. Then the real rule: never steam more than you are about to pour. What is left is not milk you saved, it is a different liquid. Its whey proteins denatured on the first heating and that does not reverse, and air folds in only while the milk is still cold, so re-steamed milk gives coarse bubbles that collapse, with the cooked note still in it. Topping cold milk up with the leftover warms the jug out of the foaming window, and raises a food safety question. Pour what you made and dump the rest."],
    ["Reading finished milk",
     "The wrong belief is that good foam stands up and a spoon should hold it. Stiff, spoonable foam is an older cappuccino style, and a guest who orders a dry cappuccino still means exactly that, but stiff foam will not pour a pattern and will not fold into the drink. What you want everywhere else is microfoam, and it looks like wet paint: a glossy, mirror-bright surface with a slight bulge, moving as one heavy liquid. If you can pick out an individual bubble, it is too big. Two moves finish it. If large bubbles are sitting on top, knock the base of the pitcher on the bench once or twice to burst them, firmly rather than hard, since a real bang drives out foam you steamed on purpose. Then swirl, and keep swirling, until the surface turns over on itself like glossy house paint being stirred. The swirl is the move that matters: foam is lighter than the milk under it and begins climbing off the moment the steam stops, and swirling folds it back in. That is the clock you are on, so do not park the pitcher and walk away. Steam, tap, swirl, wipe and purge the wand, swirl again, pour."],
    ["The pour: height, flow and the wiggle",
     "The wrong belief is that latte art comes from the wrist. The wrist only shapes white that is already floating; height and flow decide whether any white reaches the surface, and no pour saves badly textured milk. Every pattern runs the same way. (1) Swirl the pitcher until the milk is one glossy liquid, swirl the crema even, tilt the cup toward you, hold the spout high and pour a thin stream into the middle. That fall gives the stream speed to drive under the crema, so no white shows yet; white early means you are too low. (2) Somewhere between half and two thirds full, drop the spout almost onto the surface and open the flow. The stream now falls no distance and arrives with no downward speed, so the foam stays where it lands. Come down earlier for a rosetta or tulip, later for a single heart. (3) Level the cup as it fills, so nothing runs over the low rim and the whole surface opens up; hold your distance by moving the pitcher. (4) Lift and thin the stream to finish, cutting through what you made because a raised thin stream digs in again. A wiggle is side to side oscillation at a steady flow, not a speed change."],
    ["The heart is floated, not drawn",
     "The wrong belief is that a heart is drawn. Nothing is drawn. You float a circle and then cut it once, which is why most houses teach it first and why it tests your milk before your hand: foam that is stiff, bubbled or thin will not float as a clean disc, and no wrist rescues that. (1) Tilt the cup toward you, pour from height into the deep side and keep mixing until the surface is an even brown and the cup is most of the way full. (2) Drop the spout onto the surface, hold it there and open the flow up. A white disc surfaces and spreads. (3) Hold still, do not wiggle, keep pouring and let the disc push out while you bring the cup level. (4) When it nearly reaches the far rim, lift the pitcher a little, thin the stream and drive it straight through the middle of the disc and out over the far edge, then lift away. The point forms where you lift out, not where you started. The common failure is dropping in too early: with the cup still shallow the stream plunges and folds the foam under instead of leaving it on top, and the cup comes out beige."],
    ["Rosetta and tulip",
     "The wrong belief is that the rosetta and tulip are separate skills learned after the heart. Both are the heart pour with one thing added, both finish the same way, and neither appears if your foam is wrong: too thin and it sinks, too stiff and it plops. For the rosetta, everything before the white surfaces is the heart. Then, spout almost touching the surface at the far side, you wiggle: a tight side to side oscillation at steady flow while you draw the pitcher slowly back toward yourself. Each pass lays a crescent and pushes the one before it toward the far rim, so the fan opens as you retreat. At the near rim, lift, thin the stream and cut straight through it all to the far side. That line is the stem and it pulls every leaf into shape. The tulip swaps the wiggle for stops: pour a blob, cut the flow, shift back, pour a second that shoves the first away, two or three times, then cut through the lot. Stacked pulses, not one stream. Trainers differ on whether a tulip stops dead or only pauses, so follow your house. Practice off the ticket, on cheap milk or on water with a drop of dish soap, and expect blobs first."],
    ["Alternative milks, and what they do differently",
     "The wrong belief is that oat is just milk, and that non-dairy on a carton answers an allergy question. Dairy foam is held up by milk protein; most plant milks carry less, and soy, which comes close to dairy, has a different protein, so they lean on added oils, gums and stabilizers. A barista version is that milk reformulated to foam and survive espresso, so brand matters more than plant. Soy has the most protein of the three and foams stiffly, oat is what most bars find most forgiving, almond has the least and collapses fastest, though the trade disagrees on that order. Expect a shorter pour window than dairy and a cooler finish, nearer 130F to 140F, past which the foam breaks and a cooked taste comes up. Splitting is acid plus heat, soy and almond go first, so keep the shot fresh and hot, and where the build allows pour the shot into the milk rather than milk onto a standing shot. Say what you stock and that it tastes different. An allergy question is medical: some cartons reading non-dairy still contain milk-derived caseinate, and a shared wand or pitcher is cross contact, so read the carton, follow the house allergen procedure, ask a manager, and never answer from memory."],
  ] },
  { key:"filter", title:"Filter & Cold Brew", rows:[
    ["Immersion against percolation",
     "Nearly every brewer on the back bar sits in one of two families, and the family tells you more than the brand does. Immersion means the grounds sit in the whole volume of water for a set time and then you separate the two: the press, a cupping bowl, a cold brew steep. Percolation means water passes through a bed and leaves, each layer meeting liquid that already worked the layer above: pour-over, batch brew, cold drip, espresso. The wrong belief is that this is taste, like choosing a glass. Hold grind, temperature, ratio and time equal and percolation extracts more, because fresh water keeps the gradient steep while an immersion loads up and slows, which is why immersion forgives a sloppy last minute and extracts evenly, while a percolation bed works its top harder than its bottom. What the family does not decide is body. The filter does: paper strips fines and oil, mesh passes them, so a press is heavy, the same steep through paper is clean, and espresso, a percolation method, is the thickest thing you pour. Each family misses coffee its own way, channelling on one side, dry clumps and a floating crust on the other. Choose mechanism first, gear second."],
    ["Grind, and why it changes per method",
     "Grind is not one dial with strong at the fine end and weak at the coarse end, one setting for the whole bar. Size sets the rate: fine means more surface and shorter distances to travel, so extraction runs fast, and coarse runs slow. That rate has to match contact time, and that part gets taught backwards. In an immersion brewer you set the time by the clock, so grind only sets the rate: a press runs about four minutes, cold brew runs hours because cold water pulls slowly, and both stay coarse. In a pour-over or a shot the grind sets the time, because the tightness of the bed is what slows the water. A cone runs roughly 2.5 to 3.5 minutes on medium-fine, and thicker paper wants coarser and longer. Espresso takes the finest of the four, and that fineness is what holds pressurised water in the bed for the 25 to 30 seconds a shot runs. Those times are house convention, not standard. Swap them and you taste it: a fine grind given four minutes comes back bitter, drying and silty, because fines that paper would trap pass straight through mesh; a coarse grind in a portafilter is all gaps, and the shot gushes pale and sour in seconds."],
    ["Ratio, and the number to actually hold",
     "Hold one number: sixty grams of coffee to a litre of water, which is just under 1:17 by weight, not 1:16. 1:16 is 62.5 grams a litre, 1:18 is about 56, and most bars live inside that span. The Specialty Coffee Association's golden cup target sits near 55 grams a litre with room either side, and the trade disagrees past that, with Nordic charts drawing the strength band higher. Weigh it. A scoop measures volume and density is not constant: a dark roast weighs less than a light one for the same scoop, and ground packs tighter than whole bean, so the scoop swings the ratio before you touch a dial. Then hold the two axes apart. Strength is how much dissolved coffee ends up in the finished cup, measured as total dissolved solids; extraction is what percentage of the dry dose the water dissolved out. Ratio is the main lever on the first, grind, time and temperature on the second. The published filter window runs roughly 18 to 22 percent extraction at 1.15 to 1.35 percent strength. A cup can be strong and under-extracted at once, so a sour pot wants a finer grind, not a heavier dose, which only makes it thicker and sourer."],
    ["Water: temperature, and what is in it",
     "The kettle reading is not the brew temperature, and that gap is why the trade still argues about boiling water. Brew between 195 and 205F: below it the cup runs sour, thin and short; above it you pull the drying, astringent end. Grind also moves extraction, so a sour cup is a grind problem before it is a kettle problem. A kettle that has just clicked off is near 212F at sea level, over the top of the band, so give it about thirty seconds, or pour a little through the empty gear first, which drops the water and preheats at once. Treat that preheat as an extraction variable: a cold cone and carafe pull the slurry down mid-brew. Two caveats. Boiling point falls roughly a degree every five hundred feet, so a mile up the kettle already sits inside the band. And good brewers pour boiling onto light roasts deliberately, because the slurry never sees 212F. Now the water itself. Calcium and magnesium are what extraction binds to, and water stripped to nothing brews flat and is corrosive, the opposite failure from scale. Alkalinity, mostly bicarbonate, buffers acid, so high-alkalinity water flattens the brightness you paid for. Your filter is a taste decision, not only a maintenance one."],
    ["Pour-over, as a procedure",
     "Five steps, in order. (1) Rinse the paper with hot water and dump it: arguable for paper taste, not for preheating the cone. (2) Dose, level the dry bed with a tap, start a timer, pour at 195 to 205F; a small cone usually clears in two to three minutes, longer as the dose grows. (3) Bloom: wet everything with two to three times the dry weight, wait 30 to 45 seconds, because escaping carbon dioxide holds water off the grounds and stops the bed wetting evenly. A weak bloom usually means an old bag, though roast and decaf change the gas. (4) Pour in slow circles from the centre, off the paper, because water down the wall skips the bed and strands grounds high and dry. Keep the bed level and covered. (5) Swirl gently as the last pour lands, pulling the wall clean and flattening the bed for the drawdown, the last water clearing through. Then read the bed. Flat and on time is right. Stalled and soupy means too fine, or fines driven into the paper, and it drinks bitter and drying. Fast, cratered or ringed with dry grounds means too coarse, a channel, or grounds stranded above the water, and it drinks thin and sour."],
    ["The press, and what the plunge does not do",
     "The French press, the cafetière, the plunger pot: one tool, full immersion, the simplest brew going. Grind coarse, weigh about one part coffee to sixteen parts water, pour off the boil at about 200F, stir the crust down at about a minute, and steep four minutes in total. Four is a default, not a law: some guides run six to eight against a coarser grind. Skim the foam and the floaters, then press slowly and stop as the screen meets the bed, since driving it in only lifts fines back into the cup. A plunger that fights you says the grind is too fine; one that drops like a stone says too coarse. Now the part that gets missed: plunging ends nothing. The grounds sit in the same water under that screen, slowed by the packed bed but still extracting as the pot cools, which is why the second cup pours over-extracted, drying and lukewarm when the first was fine. Decant the whole pot the moment you plunge, into warmed cups or a warmed vessel, and leave the press empty. Body is the reason to use it: the mesh passes oils and fine silt that paper would have stripped. That foam is not crema, which needs a pressure you do not have here."],
    ["Batch brew is the one that pays",
     "The urn is where the coffee money is, and it is two jobs, not one. Brewing: the water has to reach the bed near 195 to 205F and hold there the whole cycle, which a brewer with a tired element will not do, and the spray head has to wet the whole bed, so read the spent grounds after, level and evenly soaked, not cratered one side and dry the other. The common fault is half-batching, a small dose in a basket built for a full one: the bed is too shallow, water runs straight through, and the pot is thin and sour. Use the half batch setting and the small basket, or brew the full batch. Holding is the other job and the one that gets skipped. Get the pot off the hotplate and into a sealed airpot you have rinsed hot first, because a hotplate does not hold coffee, it keeps cooking it: aromatics evaporate, water goes with them, and the acids break down into harsher ones, so it is not weaker, it is flat in the nose and drying at the back. Houses differ, but thirty minutes on heat and an hour sealed is the common call. Never top an old pot up with fresh. Write the brew time on it."],
    ["Cold brew and iced coffee are different drinks",
     "Two drinks, two mechanisms: the belief to kill is that cold brew is cold coffee, milder and weaker. Iced coffee is hot coffee served cold, and done properly, as flash brew or Japanese iced coffee, it counts the ice as brew water: a third to a half of your total water waits in the server as ice, the rest brewed hot over it. Hot extraction, instant chill, so the aromatics stay in, it drinks bright and acidic, and you make it to order. Cold brew never sees heat, either a coarse grind steeped 12 to 24 hours in cold or room temperature water, or percolated through a Kyoto-style tower, which is not a steep. Different compounds come out, so it reads round, sweet and quiet in the nose, though the pH gap against hot coffee is narrower than advertised. Concentrate runs 1:4 to 1:8 coffee to water by weight, cut with water at service toward drip strength, one to one from the weak end and one to three from the strong. Caffeine tracks dose and contact more than temperature, so a concentrate beats drip per ounce and your cut decides what the guest gets. It is a perishable low-acid liquid: into the cooler once filtered, dated, on a written house limit."],
  ] },
  { key:"tea", title:"Tea Service", rows:[
    ["Tea is not one temperature and one timer",
     "Your bar has one kettle, it boils, and every tea on the list gets the same water out of it. That is the failure, and green tea is where you watch it happen. A full boil, 212F at sea level, pulls the catechins and the caffeine out of a green leaf fast, and the bitterness and drying grip swamp the cup, so the guest decides they do not like green tea. The amino acids carrying that leaf's sweetness and savour come out readily in cooler water, so the cooler cup is not the thin, flavourless thing you are picturing; it is weighted differently. Japanese sencha wants roughly 160 to 175F and one to two minutes, and gyokuro, the shaded leaf, goes cooler still. Most black tea, ripe pu-erh and herbal infusions do want the full boil and three to five minutes, which is why the one-kettle habit survives: it is right for part of your list and wrong for the rest. White and oolong sit between, and the trade genuinely argues about white. So buy a variable-temperature kettle, or boil your own with the lid open, time the fall to 175F at the same fill, and write that on the wall."],
    ["The steep, and what over-steeping actually is",
     "Leaving the bag in does make the cup stronger, and that is the trap, because after the first few minutes the extra is mostly the part nobody asked for. Caffeine and the volatile aromatics come out early and fast, and the aromatics, the part that makes the tea smell the way it should, go off the top of an open cup. The polyphenols keep climbing well past that, and they bind the proteins in your saliva and read as astringency, the drying grip across the tongue, with bitterness beside it. So a black tea bag left in eight minutes is a more concentrated cup made of the wrong things. When a guest asks for stronger, give more leaf and not more time: two bags at three minutes, never one bag at seven. Scope that by leaf, because a green punishes a long steep faster than a black does, and most herbal infusions have no caffeine and little astringency to pull, so that bag can sit. Then decide who pulls it before the cup leaves your hand: pull it and send a finished cup, or send a lift-out basket with a dish and tell the table the number in seconds. What you never send is a string over the lip and no plan."],
    ["Water for tea",
     "Water is most of what you hand the guest, so treat it as an ingredient, not a utility. Draw it cold, because a hot tap line is not drinking water. Temperatures are trade convention and not a standard: green 160 to 180F and Japanese greens lower still, white 175F to just off the boil and genuinely disputed, oolong 185 to 205F, black 200 to 212F, and pu-erh and most herbal infusions at a full boil. Topping a kettle up and re-boiling it all shift concentrates what is dissolved in it, so draw fresh and boil once; the flat cup blamed on driven off gases is lore, not settled. Hardness bites both ways: very hard water dulls the cup and builds that film on a black tea, which is minerals reacting with the tea rather than dirty glassware, while water stripped of everything leaves tea thin. Carbon filtered tap sits about right; softened is not the same, since a softener trades calcium for sodium. Dose by weight, roughly 2 to 3 grams per 6 to 8 ounces, because a spoonful of fluffy white leaf and one of rolled gunpowder do not weigh the same. A bag holds about 2 grams and was built for one cup, so a 12 ounce pot wants two."],
    ["Loose leaf against the bag, honestly",
     "Loose leaf is not automatically better, and saying so gets you caught out. What actually differs is grade and room. Bags are usually filled with fannings and dust, the smallest grades, or with CTC, crush, tear and curl, which is not a leftover but a deliberate method that rolls leaf into hard granules. Either way the particles are small, so they brew fast and strong and lose aroma fast in storage, which suits a cup taken with milk and does not suit a delicate one. Whole leaf needs room to open, and the trade calls that unfurling the agony of the leaves. A flat paper bag gives it very little and a roomy sachet or pyramid gives it more, though plenty of pyramids hold dust too. So a fresh whole-leaf sachet will beat loose leaf that has sat in an open jar behind the line for a year, because freshness and room beat format. When a guest reads the price and pauses, do not apologize and do not run down the bag. Name what they are buying: the grade, the garden where there is one, a pot rather than a cup, and where the leaf allows it a second steep on fresh hot water at no extra charge."],
    ["The service itself",
     "The caddy with milk, lemon and sugar does not go with every tea, and sending it by default tells the table you were not paying attention. Warm the pot and tip the rinse out, because a cold pot drops the steep below the temperature you chose. Then the pass: pot, cup, a small dish for the bag or basket, a spoon, and the steep time said out loud or a timer on the table so the guest owns the finish. Whole leaf takes a second steep on plain hot water; a bag has little left. Now the additions. Milk is the Western default on black leaf, an assam, a ceylon, a breakfast blend, and the trade calls it wrong on green, white, oolong and a first flush darjeeling, though that is convention, not chemistry, and matcha lattes and oolong milk teas sell all day. Milk and lemon go out together only after you warn the table, because enough acid drags the cup toward pH 4.6, where casein coagulates and it curdles in front of them. Lemon also lightens a black tea's color, which is the pigments answering the acid, not a fault. Sugar sits beside the cup, never in it, and the jug lives in the fridge, not on the station."],
    ["Iced tea, and the cloudy pot",
     "Cloudy iced tea is usually not spoiled tea. The trade calls it tea cream, or creaming down: as hot tea cools, polyphenols and caffeine bind and drop out as haze, and the colder and longer you hold it, the more you see. Hard water makes it worse. Cream is reversible, so heat is your test: warm a glass of it and true cream dissolves. Haze that will not clear, or that smells sour or pours slick, is a dirty urn, and that one goes. For clarity, cold brew in the cooler overnight: it never gets hot, so it barely creams and draws less astringency. Hot double strength over ice is a speed method rather than a clarity cure. Water near boiling melts roughly its own weight of ice, so brew half your finished volume at the full leaf load and pour it over the same weight of ice, into metal not glass. Thirty-two ounces finished is sixteen ounces of double strength over about a pound of ice, weighed. It lands in the forties, so a held batch goes to the cooler and hazes there. Room temperature holding keeps an urn clear, but it is a timed hold your code may forbid. Taste the batch, because the tail of the melt is what people misjudge."],
    ["Caffeine, and the questions guests ask",
     "Say the range out loud, because leaf mass, water temperature, steep time, how finely the leaf was cut and how big the serving is move this more than the colour on the box does. Per 8 ounce cup, black commonly lands near 40 to 70 mg, oolong near 25 to 55, green near 20 to 45, and white sits anywhere across that spread, because bud is the most caffeinated part of the plant and a bud-heavy white can beat a black. The bands overlap: oxidation makes a leaf black rather than green and it neither makes nor destroys caffeine, so part of why green measures lower is that you brew it cooler and shorter. Drip coffee at the same volume sits well above any of them. Matcha runs higher than brewed green because you swallow the leaf rather than an extract of it. Decaffeinated tea is not caffeine free, so a guest avoiding caffeine on medical advice should hear trace, not none, and is better served a tisane. Tisanes are not the tea plant, so chamomile, peppermint, rooibos and hibiscus carry none, but other plants make caffeine of their own: yerba mate, guayusa and the cascara in a Cascara Fizz. And a thirty second rinse does not decaffeinate a leaf."],
    ["Tea on a bar programme",
     "Two or three grams makes a cup and costs cents, so the reflex is to price tea under coffee and treat the tin as immortal. Both halves are wrong. Brewed tea does not hold: leaf left in the pot keeps extracting and turns astringent, and it goes flat and hazy as it cools, so pull the leaf. Dry leaf keeps, but it ages. Green fades fastest and tastes tired inside a year, black holds longer, and the teas built to age are the post-fermented dark ones, pu-erh among them, along with some roasted oolong and pressed white, though the trade disagrees about how to store them. Keep working tins airtight, opaque, cool, dry and away from smells, because dry leaf is a superb sponge. Never park one by ground coffee or the dish machine, and keep it out of the fridge, which makes it sweat when opened cold. Your waste is the brewed batch, not the leaf. Iced tea held at room temperature is a food safety item: your local code sets the hours and the discard, and the urn and spigot need a cleaning schedule. On price you are selling the seat and the server's time, because a pot goes back for hot water, so price it level with the coffee."],
  ] },
  { key:"leaf", title:"The Leaf", rows:[
    ["One plant, six teas",
     "Every real tea on your back bar comes off one species, Camellia sinensis, and the wrong belief to kill first is that white, green and black grow on different bushes. They do not. Hibiscus and rooibos are not tea at all. Two varieties are worth naming: var. sinensis, the small-leaf China bush suited to cooler high ground, and var. assamica, the large-leaf tropical one behind most Assam, Yunnan and East African tea. Everything else is what somebody did to the picked leaf. The Chinese system counts six classes: white (bai cha), green (lu cha), yellow (huang cha), oolong (wulong, filed as qing cha, blue-green tea), black (hong cha) and dark (hei cha). That last pair is the trap. What you call black tea is hong cha, red tea, named for the liquor's colour, not the leaf, while hei cha, literally black tea, is the pile-fermented family where microbes genuinely do the work. The other five are not fermented; a hong cha darkens because the bruised leaf's own enzymes meet air, and that is oxidation. Pu-erh sits in that dark corner, but the trade argues it, since China gives pu-erh its own standard and a raw green-style cake is nothing like the piled one. So when a supplier writes black or dark, ask which."],
    ["Oxidation is the axis",
     "Oxidation is not fermentation, and calling it that is the standard error on tea lists. You know oxidation as a wine fault; on a leaf it is the point. Bruise or roll a leaf and you rupture its cells, letting its own enzymes, polyphenol oxidase chiefly, meet its catechins in air. Those catechins oxidise onward into theaflavins and thearubigins, which give a black tea its colour, its body and its brisk edge. No yeast, no bacteria, no sugar going to alcohol, with one exception below. The craft is choosing when to stop it, and heat does that: the kill-green step, shaqing, done by pan in China and by steam, mushi, in Japan. Now map the six. Green is killed early, so it stays bright and vegetal. White is withered and dried, never rolled, with no kill-green of its own, so it oxidises lightly across a long wither, though the trade still splits on whether to call that unoxidised. Oolong is bruised at the leaf edge and stopped partway. Black is rolled and carried far before firing ends it. Two sit off the axis: yellow is green plus a smothered damp rest, men huang, run after the enzyme is dead, and dark is genuinely microbial, which is its own lesson."],
    ["Where the leaf comes from",
     "The wrong belief is that the name on the tin is a place. English Breakfast and Irish Breakfast are blend recipes with no legal meaning, the Irish one conventionally maltier rather than dependably stronger, and chai is simply the word for tea. What you can name to a guest with a straight face: Darjeeling, in the West Bengal hills, thin and floral in first flush, grapey in second, later flushes after that, and carrying a geographical indication, so the word is legally defined; Assam, a hot river valley, malty and thick, the backbone of a builder's cup; Nilgiri further south; Sri Lanka, still traded as Ceylon, graded by elevation, low, medium and high grown, the low heavy and dark, the high brisk and bright; Kenya and the East African belt, almost all CTC, the machine-cut method that fills tea bags, against orthodox whole leaf; Fujian for white tea, traditional jasmine scenting, smoked lapsang souchong and, at Wuyi and Anxi, two oolong benchmarks; Yunnan for pu-erh and gold-tipped black; Taiwan for gaoshan, high mountain oolong. Japan is the split worth carrying: nearly all its green is steamed rather than pan-fired, so it keeps a marine edge where a Chinese green browns toward toasted chestnut. That is processing, not latitude."],
    ["Grades, and what they do not tell you",
     "Orange pekoe is not a flavour, not a type and not a mark of quality, and the guest who orders one means plain black tea. The letters grade the dry leaf on size and on how much tip, the unopened bud, it carries, nothing else. The orthodox ladder runs OP for a whole twisted leaf, FOP, flowery, once tip shows, then GFOP, TGFOP, FTGFOP and SFTGFOP stacking on golden, tippy, finest and special. Below whole leaf sit the broken grades, BOP, then fannings, BOPF, then dust, none of them insults. Smaller particles carry more surface, so they give up colour and strength fast, which is why a bag and an iced tea urn want them and whole leaf does not suit either. Two things the ladder never tells you. No regulator polices the letters, so each estate assigns its own. And it is read off the dry leaf, never the cup, so a heavily tippy lot can pour light and sweet while a plain BOP drinks brisk. The letters are Indian and Sri Lankan practice above all; Chinese and Japanese tea is sorted by harvest, cultivar and leaf shape. Taste before you put it on a list. You will hear both peck-oh and pee-koh, and the orange has nothing to do with citrus."],
    ["Pu-erh and the genuinely fermented teas",
     "Fermentation is the wrong word for black tea and oolong, which only oxidize. Hei cha, the dark family, is the exception among the six: microbes really do the work, on purpose. Yunnan pu-erh starts as sun-dried large-leaf green called maocha, then forks. Sheng, raw, is usually pressed into cakes and left for years to turn from astringent and green into woody and dried-fruited, though whether that is microbes or slow oxidation is argued, and humid storage tilts it toward microbes. Shu, ripe, is the modern shortcut and the undisputed case: damp leaf heaped warm in a room, wo dui, turned for weeks, tasting of the cellar by design. Liu bao from Guangxi belongs here, and the Hunan brick whose golden bloom, jin hua, is a cultivated fungus, not a fault. The wrong belief is that the funk is spoilage, so sell it as you sell an aged rum. Ammonia or wet cardboard is bad storage, not age, and that cake stays in the tin. Rinse the leaf a few seconds and tip that water out, then brew short and often, 212F for shu and aged cakes and a touch cooler for young sheng. Say forest floor, damp wood, dark chocolate, betel nut. Never say musty. Pour it after a heavy meal."],
    ["Tisanes are not tea",
     "Chamomile is a flower, peppermint is a leaf, rooibos is a South African shrub, and not one of them is tea. The trade word is tisane, or herbal infusion, and the guest will keep saying herbal tea, so correct the answer not the guest. Caffeine first. A guest who asks for herbal usually means none, and behind that question there is often a pregnancy or a medication, so do not guess. Rooibos, honeybush, chamomile, peppermint, lemon verbena, ginger and hibiscus carry none. Yerba mate, guayusa, yaupon, guarana and cascara, the dried coffee cherry, all do, and cascara is in your zero-proof list right now, as is kombucha, which is brewed from tea and no tisane at all. Decaffeinated tea is reduced, not emptied. Allergies second. Chamomile sits in the daisy family, so a guest with a ragweed allergy has a real cross-reaction risk, and a blend can carry a dozen botanicals where a straight tisane carries one, so hand over the box and read the ingredient list rather than answering from memory. Caffeine free is not the same as safe, and that second call is not yours. Stock chamomile, peppermint, rooibos, ginger and hibiscus and you cover nearly every ask, and hibiscus earns its slot twice over, tart and red enough to mix with."],
  ] },
  { key:"matcha", title:"Matcha & Chai", rows:[
    ["Matcha is not green tea put through a grinder",
     "The wrong belief is that matcha is green tea put through a grinder. It is a green tea, so do not tell a guest otherwise, but ground sencha is powder and not matcha. Matcha starts in the field a few weeks before picking, when the bushes are covered, lightly then heavily. Starved of light the leaf builds chlorophyll and keeps theanine, which sunlight drives down as catechins build, so it reads darker, sweeter and more savoury than astringent. Shade alone is not the line, because gyokuro is shaded too and still rolled and steeped. That leaf is steamed, dried flat without rolling and stripped of stem and vein, which is tencha. Milling tencha makes matcha, traditionally on a slow granite stone that is much of the price, though cheap powder is machine milled. Then the real break. You do not steep it, you suspend it, so strength is set by the powder you whisk, not by what water pulls from a leaf, and the bowl is part solution, part suspension. It settles. A standing bowl goes gritty at the bottom, and iced matcha separates on the way to the table, so whisk late and walk fast. The calmer lift guests ask about is reported rather than proven, so say so."],
    ["Grades of matcha, and the one you buy",
     "Ceremonial and culinary are marketing words. No law defines them, no body certifies them, and nothing stops a supplier printing ceremonial on late-harvest dust. What sits underneath is real: first harvest against later pickings, how long the field was shaded, whether stem and vein were stripped out, and whether it was stone-ground or run through a fast mill that can heat the aroma off. The top end exists for usucha, thin tea, and for koicha, thick tea, which is kneaded rather than whisked and barely diluted, so a lesser powder has nowhere to hide. Put that grade in a sweetened twelve ounce latte and you have paid for an aroma the milk erases; go the other way and cheap powder turns khaki and bitter behind the sugar. Since the words mean nothing, buy on evidence: ask the harvest date, then taste it in milk before you commit. Treat colour as a veto, not a verdict: dull yellow-brown is old or heat-damaged, vivid jade only proves it is not. Buy small tins, keep them airtight, cool and dark, and let a chilled tin reach room temperature before opening or condensation clumps and dulls it. Once open it has weeks of useful life, not months."],
    ["Whisking, and why it clumps",
     "Matcha clumps because of what it is, not because you whisk too politely. Ground that fine, the powder is cohesive and pulls water from the air, so it sits in the tin already lumped, and each lump wets on the outside and seals dry powder in, so one big pour leaves grit no stirring reopens. The fix is mechanical, not muscular. (1) Sift it through fine mesh into the bowl, the chawan, every time. (2) Water well off the boil, around 175F for thin tea, usucha, though guidance runs roughly 160F to 180F. Hotter water scorches nothing; it dissolves more of the bitter, drying catechins, while the sweet amino acids come out even in cool water, so the balance tips harsh. (3) Paste first, a splash worked against the side into a smooth slurry, then the rest. (4) Whisk from the wrist. Soak the bamboo whisk, the chasen, in warm water so the tines flex rather than snap, drive it in a brisk W just off the floor, then one slow circle at the surface to sink coarse bubbles. It is a suspension, not a solution, so it goes out now; a clumped bowl finishes as sludge. Rinse it in water only, never soap or a machine, then stand it to dry."],
    ["The matcha latte on a bar",
     "Build the matcha as a base of its own, like a shot. Matcha never dissolves, hot or cold. It is whole leaf ground fine and held in suspension, so powder tipped straight into milk clumps, drags and settles out gritty. Sift it, then whisk about two grams, near enough a level teaspoon, into two ounces of water at around 175F, in a fast W rather than circles, until it foams: that is usucha, thin tea. Keep the water off the boil, because hotter water pulls more catechin and reads more bitter. Then six to eight ounces of milk, steamed a shade under your latte temperature. Sweeten with syrup, since granulated sugar will not go in, and harder than feels right, because unsweetened matcha in milk reads to most guests as grassy milk. Latte art still works, because the green base gives the contrast crema gives on espresso, so pour the milk into the matcha and the pattern sits on green. Pouring green over milk is the iced build instead: the same base over milk and ice, or the powder shaken with cold water in a sealed tin, which works but takes longer. If the colour disappoints, it is grade, then age, then how much milk sits over how little powder."],
    ["Chai is a recipe, not an ingredient",
     "Chai means tea. That is the entire word in Hindi, Urdu, Russian, Turkish and Swahili, all of them the Chinese word arriving overland, while English tea is the same word arriving by sea. So chai tea says tea tea, and a guest ordering a chai has technically ordered tea. What they mean is masala chai, spiced tea, a recipe in four parts: strong black tea, usually Assam and usually CTC, the machine cut leaf, because it gives colour and briskness fast enough to stand up to dairy; milk; sugar, boiled in rather than offered on the table; and the masala, most often green cardamom leading, then ginger, cinnamon or cassia, clove and black pepper, with fennel, nutmeg or star anise as house choices. Kadak means strong. The recipe shifts by household and by street like a family sauce, so there is no correct version to memorise. Concentrate is a carton of spiced brew, usually sweetened, cut with milk to order: a product, not the drink. A dirty chai is a chai latte with espresso through it; some bars call a double shot a filthy. A milk batch is dairy, not tea: hold it at 135F or above, or build to order. Ask which one a guest means before you build."],
    ["Making chai properly, and the shortcut",
     "The wrong belief is that chai is spiced tea you steep like a tea bag, and that boiling it is a fault. Masala chai is a decoction, boiled on purpose. Two things make it work. Milk protein binds tannin, so tea pulled hard enough to be harsh in water reads round in the cup, which is why the brisk crush, tear and curl grades, CTC, belong here and a delicate first flush is wasted. And much of what you want from cardamom, clove, cinnamon and pepper sits in oil, so whole milk holds it where an open pot lets the volatiles boil off. Water alone is not flavourless, only thinner and sharper. The method: (1) crack whole spices, not powder, which goes gritty, and simmer them in water a few minutes; (2) add tea for two or three minutes; (3) add milk and sugar, bring it back up, letting it rise and drop more than once; (4) strain. Order varies by house. The bar shortcut is to batch one and two, hold that milk-free concentrate at or above 135F, or at or below 41F, and cut it with steamed milk to order. Never hold it with the milk already in. Whether that batch is still house masala chai is the owner's call."],
  ] },
  { key:"spiked", title:"Spiked & Service", rows:[
    ["The pre-heat, and handing it over",
     "The wrong belief is that pre-heating is only about cracking, so a sturdy mug that will never break can skip it. It is also a service step, and on a mug that half is the bigger one: what steals heat is the vessel's mass against the four ounces you pour in, and a heavy mug carries far more than a thin glass, so a big mug holding a small drink is the worst pairing. The rest is true too: once warmed, that mass holds heat better than thin glass, so the mug is the worse thief at the pour and the better keeper after. (1) Give a heavy mug a long soak or the machine's cup tray, not the quick rinse a thin glass gets, (2) dump it and dry the outside before you pour, (3) stir the sugar in until the spoon finds no grit, since cold is not what leaves it there, sugar dissolving cold as well, only slower. Leave the rim clear, set the drink down rather than passing it hand to hand, because a hot vessel changing grip is the one that gets dropped, carry a stemmed glass by its stem, and say hot out loud. Houses disagree on where the handle points, so follow yours."],
    ["The cream float, and why it fails",
     "The wrong belief is that the float is a garnish and that stiffer cream is safer. It is neither. The float is a density gap, and heat works against you, since hot coffee is lighter than cold cream and on its own would take it down. Two things build it back. Sugar, because dissolved sugar makes the coffee denser, so the sugar you skipped is the float you lost. Air, because whipping cuts the cream's density far more than fat alone can, and holding air needs roughly 30 percent milkfat as a floor, which is light whipping cream, while heavy cream at 36 percent or more gives margin. Half-and-half at 10.5 to under 18 percent neither whips nor floats, and aerosol cream floats, then collapses to a slick. Keep it under 50F or the fat is too soft to take air, and whip only to where a ribbon off the whisk holds a second before sinking back. Stiff cream still floats, so that one is a service fault, not a flotation fault: it lands as a scoop the guest eats off the top. Fill to about half an inch below the rim, pour slowly over a spoon touching the surface, and tell the guest to drink through it, not stir it."],
    ["Espresso in a cocktail",
     "The wrong belief is that the crown is a shaking trick, so any coffee will do if you shake hard enough. The mirror error is worse: the crema does not ride through, because shaking destroys it. What you serve is air, whipped in by the ice and held by two things. Concentration first: espresso carries several times the dissolved solids of cold brew, and those solids are the surfactants holding a bubble wall together, so an ounce of espresso does what an ounce of cold brew cannot. Sugar second: the liqueur and the syrup thicken the drink and slow the foam draining back out, which is why a head shaken without them sinks fast. Gas from a fresh shot adds to that, though it is the smallest part, and a shot that shakes up flat anyway points at the bag date. So pull the shot last, straight into the tin, shake hard on plenty of hard ice, fine strain so chips cannot pit the surface, then leave it alone while the foam climbs. A hot shot melts more ice and the spec assumes that dilution. Whether pre-chilling costs height is argued both ways, so if volume forces you to batch, keep the sugar, shake harder, and promise a head rather than a crema."],
    ["Coffee liqueur is not coffee",
     "The wrong belief is that coffee liqueur is coffee in a bottle, so it trades one for one with a shot and carries no caffeine. It is a sweetened spirit flavoured with coffee, most bottles 20 to 25 percent alcohol, outliers from 16 to 35, and in most the sugar talks louder than the roast. So the swap is never even. Swap an ounce of liqueur for an ounce of espresso and you have removed sweetness and alcohol and added acidity, bitterness and heat: rebalance from about a half ounce of syrup, and decide whether to chill that shot or let it melt your ice, which the trade argues about. Reverse it and you have built a dessert. Then ask three things, never guess: hot or cold; sweet and creamy or strong and bitter, which sorts the liqueur drinks from the ones built on a real shot; and whether they want the caffeine, because the late diner wants none and the espresso martini guest usually wants it. Never answer none. A sugar led bottle carries a few milligrams an ounce against dozens in a shot, a cold brew led one carries more, and no mainstream bottle is decaf, so the guest who wants the flavour without the lift needs a decaf shot instead."],
    ["Counting a spiked coffee",
     "The wrong belief is that a spiked coffee counts for less because it is hot, sweet and served like dessert. Concede the true half: sipped slowly, with cream and sugar slowing the stomach, the same ethanol arrives later and flatter than a shot, which is why nobody at the table notices the total climbing. The total is what you count. An Irish coffee built on an ounce and a half of forty percent whiskey is one standard drink, the same as that pour neat, so four of them is six ounces of whiskey against a table that says it has had a couple of coffees. Count ounces times proof, not ounces of liquid: the Spanish coffee's two ounces come off three bottles, and its flamed overproof rum, commonly a 151, is nearly a standard drink in three quarters of an ounce while the coffee liqueur beside it is about half the strength of whiskey. So jigger every mug and ring it in like a shot. Caffeine changes none of that. It clears the drowsiness, which was the brake rather than the impairment, so the guest ordering one to sober up buys alertness and reads it as sobriety. The legal section covers why that fails. Pour the Sober Irish Coffee and a glass of water."],
    ["Running coffee and tea through a bar shift",
     "The wrong belief is that adding coffee means buying a machine. It means opening a second station that lives or dies on mise. Set it like a well: mugs and glasses within reach of hot water, cream whipped light, covered and cold, whisked a few turns before each float because it separates as it stands, firming on top while serum drains beneath, a demerara or brown sugar syrup that jiggers to the same sweetness every time, teas in tins carrying their temperature and time, a timer, and a cloth for the steam wand that touches nothing else. Then split the list in two. Hold: syrups, chai and tea concentrate, cold brew, and that cream, dated. Make to order: every shot, every steamed milk, every whisked matcha, because each comes apart the moment it is made, aromatics off the shot, drainage in the milk, matcha settling out of suspension. Purge the grinder and dump a shot before the first order, because the coffees land late on a stale retained dose. Should you sell it at all? Only if somebody owns it. The trade is split on whether an espresso martini needs a live shot, so settle that in writing, and if nobody does, carry cold brew and a good liqueur instead."],
  ] },
  { key:"faults", title:"Faults", rows:[
    ["Start with the last thing that changed",
     "The wrong belief is that a bad cup is a puzzle about extraction, so you reach for the grinder first. Grind is the right dial once you know extraction moved, but a poor opening move: find what changed since the last good drink. Walk it in that order: (1) your own hands, the newest variable here, since a channelled puck moves a shot further than the next bag of the same coffee; (2) the grinder, a refill, a near empty hopper, somebody else's adjustment, the dose weighed not guessed; (3) the bean, a new bag or a stale one; (4) the machine, what the gauge does during the shot and whether the shower screen is clean; (5) the milk, the jug, the wand, the carton; (6) the vessel, because detergent or rinse aid left on a cup flattens crema and foam. Water sits outside the walk: it moves over weeks, not between two drinks, so schedule it. Then change one thing, purge the grinder before you taste, and write the setting down. Trainers split on running that test mid rush against resetting to the setting that worked, which only helps if it was written down. A grind change made to cure a dirty portafilter leaves you two problems and no memory of either."],
    ["The cup tastes like the last drink",
     "The wrong belief is that coffee is loud enough to bury whatever it meets. Most of what reads as taste is aroma, taint is aromatic, and coffee oil dissolves and holds volatiles rather than covering them, so carryover lands first. Let the drink narrow the path. If black coffee is wrong too, suspect the shared path: a group left unflushed on old grounds and rancid oil, a grinder holding the last beans, or a cup wearing glasswasher rinse aid. If only milk drinks are wrong, suspect the wand, which draws milk back inside and needs purging as well as wiping, or a jug rinsed, not washed. If only iced drinks are wrong, suspect the well, where cut citrus and onion reach the ice; a cocktail would hide it. Test in that order: smell the dry cup, then hot water stood in it, then the dry jug and a fresh dose, then plain water through the group. Taste your cold filtered water first, since a spent filter tastes like a dirty group. Two are not flavour faults: dairy reaching a drink sold as non-dairy, and regular beans left in a chute dosing a decaf. Flavoured beans earn their own grinder, and after a bean change purge until what falls smells like the new bag."],
    ["Bitter is not one thing",
     "Bitter is what a guest says when a coffee is unpleasant, and it lands on sour cups as often as bitter ones, so taste it yourself first: four separate faults hide under the word. (1) A harsh, drying shot that reads fine and turns rough after you swallow. That drying is astringency, a texture rather than a taste, and an uneven puck causes it as often as a long extraction, which part of the trade doubts you can taste at all, so check prep and yield before the grinder. (2) Roast bitterness, there from the first sip, carbon and ash rather than dryness, follows the bag and not the machine, so it is on the filter brewer too and gone with the bag. In a dark roast built for milk it is no fault, and since milk mutes bitterness, a bitter latte points at the roast or the machine, not your grind. (3) Overheated or re-steamed milk, cooked and faintly eggy over thin foam, never caramel, since nothing caramelises at steaming heat. (4) Rancid oil from a dirty grinder, basket or group, stale in the empty cup and as loud in an Americano as a latte. Taste group water first, then the shot, then the milk."],
    ["Sour, and the word the guest will use instead",
     "Take the guest's word as a complaint, not a diagnosis. A cup that is under-extracted rarely comes back called sour; it comes back bitter, or strong, or burnt, because unpleasant coffee gets filed under bitter by most people. So pull another and taste it yourself: you cannot read a shot through a milk drink. Then fork three ways. Ran fast and went pale early: resistance is low, so look at grind, at a dose too small for its basket, which leaves headspace the puck collapses into, and at beans fresh enough to still be gassing and blowing the puck open. Finished on time but hollow and harsh at once: that is channelling, which can also run fast but is the only one of the three that passes a timer. Finished on time and merely flat and sour: leave the grinder alone and look at a portafilter left off the group, a machine worked flat through a rush, or coffee roasted light enough to want more than your setting gives. Whether a hopper refill or hot burrs drift coarse or fine is argued and differs by grinder, so trust the shot over the theory. Then remake it, and do not argue about the word."],
    ["Staleness, and the three clocks",
     "The wrong belief is that freshness is one question the bag answers. Three clocks run at once and only the first is printed. The roast clock runs in weeks, slowed behind a one-way valve and much faster once the bag is open or the beans sit in a warm lit hopper, half the Monday drift you blame on the grinder. The grind clock runs in minutes, because grinding opens the cell structure and the escaping gas carries the aroma with it, so grind to order and keep no pre-ground. The brewed clock is fastest, since heat, air and evaporation work at once: an open decanter on a hotplate stews woody and sharp inside about half an hour, and a sealed thermal server buys longer. Brew smaller, more often, and write the time on it. Then tell it apart. Staleness is an absence, papery and dull, a cup that tastes of nothing rather than of something wrong, and the proof is that the grinder cannot chase it, since finer turns bitter and coarser turns thin. Rancid is a taste that arrived and points at the hopper and burrs; sour and sharp is extraction. Thin crema is a hint, not proof, because robusta in a blend throws crema regardless."],
    ["Milk faults",
     "The wrong belief is that every milk problem is a steaming problem, and that re-steaming rescues a jug. Once milk is at serving temperature the whey proteins that build foam are spent and more steam only adds heat; milk caught still cold can be stretched. Scalded milk smells boiled and eggy, sulphur off cooked protein, and its foam collapses thin. Split milk shows grain that catches on the spoon and rings the cup, from near-date milk or acid in the drink; heat multiplies both and causes neither. Flat milk is hot liquid with no microfoam, from a tip held under the surface, a jug that came to you warm or soapy, or a wand left blocked or unpurged. Over-aerated milk is stiff and pours in blobs, from air added too long or too late; it drains to a dry cap, separation without grain and not a split. Old milk smells sour cold, so smell it cold before you blame your hand. Big bubbles on glossy milk swirl back; flat milk has nothing to fold in. Scalded, split and old go down the drain and the drink is remade. Cooked milk is the point of a Thai iced tea, and splitting it on purpose makes a clarified milk punch."],
    ["Water faults, and how to tell them from the grinder",
     "Hard hides two numbers that fault a cup in opposite directions, so stop treating it as one. Alkalinity is the buffering half, and it neutralises the acids while you brew: the cup lands flat, chalky and muted with whatever bitterness is there left standing alone, and no grind change moves it. Hardness is the calcium and magnesium half, and those ions help pull flavour out of the grounds, so hard water low in alkalinity brews heavy and sharp rather than dull. Reverse osmosis strips both for the opposite fault, thin, hollow and sour, with nothing to buffer and nothing to extract, and aggressive enough on brass that it is blended back up rather than run neat. Chlorine reads as swimming pool; chlorine on coffee's phenols reads as medicinal, loudest in tea and light filter. Many supplies now run chloramine instead, which a plain carbon block removes far more slowly than chlorine, so a fitted filter is no promise the taste has gone. A spent cartridge stops removing rather than hands anything back, and the white crust at your steam tip is dried solids from evaporation, not evidence, so change on rated throughput. Then the check that saves you: a water fault does not move when you move the grind."],
    ["The complaint at the pass",
     "Check the ticket against the cup first: a guest who ordered decaf, or oat, and got neither has a safety problem, not a flavour one. (1) Take it back, say you will sort it, offer no theory. (2) Ask one question you can act on, what would you change, and treat strong as the trap word: it means concentrated to one guest and harsh to the next, and those take opposite fixes, a longer drink against a coarser grind. If the answer is hotter, milk has a ceiling, so warm the cup and move faster rather than scald it. (3) Do not drink from the cup they handed back, since most codes and many houses forbid it, and a cup that has stood exaggerates its own fault as it cools and falls flat. Better evidence is free: pull a shot to waste, or check how long the batch stood. (4) One bad cup with sound ones either side is that cup alone, so remake and taste your own work. Two means the grind, the bag or the machine, so stop and tell the bar. A comp is a manager's call in most houses, not yours. The wrong belief is that a complaint is about one cup. Everyone who said nothing drank the same thing."],
  ] },
];

/* Reference cards, filed by `dom` against a section key above. A card whose
   dom names no section renders nowhere and searches to a dead route, which is
   why tools/check.mjs closes that loop. */
const COFFEE_REF = [
  /* The drinks a bar actually sells. Filed under the section that explains
     them: the black ones under The Shot, the milk ones under Milk & Latte
     Art, because that is where a reader who does not know the difference
     between a cortado and a flat white will be standing. */
  { dom:"shot", name:"Espresso", cat:"Black", note:"Espresso is not a bean and not a roast level. It is a method: roughly nine bar of pressure driven through a fine, tamped bed. Any coffee can be pulled as espresso, and a bag stamped espresso roast is a roaster's suggestion, not a category. So when a guest asks what espresso you use, they are asking about the bean. Answer with the origin and the roast.",
    facts:[["Ratio","18 to 20 g in, 36 to 40 g out on the scale, 25 to 30 seconds, for the modern double. The older Italian single runs about 7 g for roughly 25 mL in the cup, and that is a volume including the crema rather than a weight. The range comes from basket size and roast: light roasts are harder to extract and usually want a finer grind and the long end of the pull."], ["Volume","1.2 to 1.4 oz (36 to 40 mL) for the double, weighed rather than eyeballed, because the crema makes the cup look fuller than the number; a traditional single is about 0.85 oz (25 mL)"], ["Milk","None"], ["Glass","Warmed demitasse, 2 to 3 oz, taken off the top of the machine where it has been sitting hot"], ["Serve","Brew water 195 to 205F, and it lands in the cup well below that. Spoon on the saucer, a glass of still water beside it, and walk it out the moment it is pulled, because crema is a foam and it thins as it stands"], ["Tastes like","Syrupy and dense under the crema, sweet through the middle, a bitter edge at the back; citrus and berry from light roasts, cocoa and nut from dark"], ["Sells to","After dinner, to the guest killing ten minutes at the bar, and to every cook and server before and after a shift. In Italy this is what a guest means by caffè, with no other word attached"]] },
  { dom:"shot", name:"Ristretto", cat:"Black", note:"Ristretto means restricted, not strong. You cut the shot early, keeping the acids and sugars that come out first and leaving more of the bitter compounds in the puck. From the same dose that means slightly less total caffeine than a full shot, not more. It tastes more intense only because it is more concentrated per sip, which is a different claim.",
    facts:[["Ratio","Same dose as your double, 18 to 20 g in, but 25 to 30 g out, which is a quarter to a third less liquid from the same coffee. Cut a normal grind early and it runs 20 to 25 seconds; grind finer for it, as some houses do, and it takes the same 25 to 30 seconds as a full shot."], ["Volume","About 0.85 to 1 oz (25 to 30 mL)"], ["Milk","None"], ["Glass","Warmed demitasse, 2 to 3 oz. It will sit barely a third full, and it is meant to"], ["Serve","Brew water 195 to 205F. Straight over with no water back unless the guest asks, because it is two swallows and nobody lingers over it"], ["Tastes like","Thick and sweet up front, bright acidity, a short finish, and noticeably less bitter than a full shot. Syrup rather than coffee"], ["Sells to","To the guest who says espresso is too bitter, and as the base shot in a piccolo. Some Australasian bars build a flat white on it too, though what a flat white is remains an open argument in the trade"]] },
  { dom:"shot", name:"Lungo", cat:"Black", note:"A lungo is not an Americano. Every drop goes through the bed under pressure, so you are extracting further into the puck rather than diluting a finished shot, which is where the bitterness and the drying finish come from. It is more dilute in the cup than an espresso and carries more total caffeine than one, from the same dose. Thinner per sip is not less coffee.",
    facts:[["Ratio","18 to 20 g in, 50 to 60 g out, roughly 30 to 45 seconds. Some houses push out to 70 g. The range is how far you are willing to go before the cup turns hollow and drying, so taste the far end before you set it."], ["Volume","1.7 to 2 oz (50 to 60 mL), and nearer 2.4 oz (70 mL) where the house pulls to the far end"], ["Milk","None"], ["Glass","Warmed cup at the 3 oz end of your demitasse range, filled higher than an espresso"], ["Serve","Brew water 195 to 205F. Goes out alone; there is enough liquid in it that a water back is not expected"], ["Tastes like","Thinner and longer than espresso, more bitterness, a drying papery finish, and the bean's darker notes pulled forward over its fruit"], ["Sells to","To the guest who wants espresso to last more than two sips but will not accept an Americano, and to capsule machine drinkers used to a button marked lungo"]] },
  { dom:"shot", name:"Americano", cat:"Black", note:"An Americano is a shot lengthened with hot water poured onto it, and that incoming water breaks the crema, which is what separates it from a long black built in the other order. The bigger thing to hold: a large Americano is not a stronger coffee. It carries exactly the caffeine of the shots in it, so when a guest wants more, sell another shot rather than a bigger cup.",
    facts:[["Ratio","A double, 18 to 20 g in and 36 to 40 g out in 25 to 30 seconds, then 4 to 6 oz of hot water poured on after. That is about 1 part espresso to 3 parts water in a small cup and closer to 1 to 4 or 5 in a large one, and the range is cup size and how the guest drinks it."], ["Volume","About 5.25 to 7.5 oz (155 to 220 mL) finished"], ["Milk","None. Milk and cream go on the side and the guest decides"], ["Glass","Warmed 8 oz cup or mug. For an iced one use a cold glass and plenty of ice, never a cup off the top of the machine"], ["Serve","Hot water straight off the machine's tap, which on many machines runs closer to boiling than to brew temperature, so this lands hotter than any milk drink and may genuinely need a minute. Leave half an inch of headroom so it carries and so there is room for milk"], ["Tastes like","Clean and long, closer to filter coffee than to espresso, with the shot's body still under it and the crema broken up by the pour rather than sitting as a layer"], ["Sells to","All day, to the black coffee drinker when you have no brewer running, and to anyone who found the drip pot empty at two in the afternoon"]] },
  { dom:"shot", name:"Long Black", cat:"Black", note:"The wrong belief is that a long black is just an Americano with an accent. Water into the cup first, shot pulled onto it, and the crema arrives as a layer the guest drinks through instead of being broken by the pour. The second difference is size: less water on the same shot, so it stays denser to the bottom. How much survives past the first sip is argued about, so taste both yourself.",
    facts:[["Ratio","Hot water first, 3 to 4 oz of it, then a double pulled straight onto it, 18 to 20 g in for 36 to 40 g out in 25 to 30 seconds. Where the house pulls ristretto instead, the same dose gives 25 to 30 g out and a denser cup. Australian and New Zealand practice sits tighter and shorter than an Americano."], ["Volume","About 4.25 to 5.5 oz (125 to 165 mL) finished, deliberately smaller than an Americano"], ["Milk","None"], ["Glass","Warmed 6 oz cup, about three quarters full on the short build and near the brim on the long one"], ["Serve","Hot water off the tap into the cup first, hotter than brew temperature on most machines, then the shot pulled directly onto it, and out immediately while the crema is still whole. Like the Americano it arrives very hot, so do not rush the guest into the first sip"], ["Tastes like","Fuller and more aromatic than an Americano on the first sip, because you drink through the crema; the bitterness arrives later as it settles"], ["Sells to","To Australian and New Zealand guests, who mostly arrive with a fixed idea of it, and to anyone who wants an Americano with more of the shot left in it"]] },
  { dom:"milk", name:"Macchiato", cat:"With Milk", note:"Macchiato means stained, and it is the espresso being stained, not the milk. A latte macchiato inverts it: a tall glass of steamed milk marked with the shot last, layered and pale. The flavored syrup drink on a chain menu is a third thing, closer to a vanilla latte. So ask which one the guest means, because the three arrive in different glasses at different sizes.",
    facts:[["Ratio","A double, 18 to 20 g in, 36 to 40 g out, 25 to 30 seconds, then marked with milk. In Italy the caffè macchiato is usually built on a single instead. The range is simply whether your bar owns a single basket or runs doubles only."], ["Volume","1.7 to 2.4 oz (50 to 70 mL)"], ["Milk","About 0.5 to 1 oz, and mostly foam. Stretch a small jug, let it settle a moment, then spoon or drop a single dollop onto the middle of the shot. Silky and wet, never stiff, and it should sit as a visible mark rather than stirring itself in"], ["Glass","Warmed demitasse at the 3 oz end, since a double marked with a full ounce of milk crowds a 2 oz cup"], ["Serve","Milk at 140 to 150F, the shot fresh underneath it, and out inside thirty seconds so the mark is still a mark and not a cloud"], ["Tastes like","Espresso with the sharp edge knocked off, a soft dairy note through the middle, and a finish that is still clearly coffee"], ["Sells to","To the guest who finds espresso too harsh but will not be seen ordering a cappuccino, and in Italy as the usual way to take milk after a meal, where a cappuccino is a morning drink"]] },
  { dom:"milk", name:"Cortado", cat:"With Milk", note:"Cortar means to cut, and the milk is there to cut the acid, not to make a small latte. What separates them is texture and heat: barely any foam, and milk steamed cooler on purpose so the drink is at drinking temperature the moment it lands. Steam it to latte heat and latte texture and you have built a four ounce latte in a nice glass, which is a different drink.",
    facts:[["Ratio","A double, 18 to 20 g in, 36 to 40 g out, 25 to 30 seconds, then milk. There are two builds behind the one name and they are not the same drink. The Spanish cut is a single with roughly its own volume again in milk, about 1 to 1. The American Gibraltar build puts a double under milk to fill the glass, which lands nearer 1 to 2, because there the glass sets the milk and not the ratio."], ["Volume","2 to 3 oz (60 to 90 mL) for the Spanish cut, 4 to 4.5 oz (120 to 135 mL) filled into a Gibraltar"], ["Milk","About 1 to 1.5 oz behind a single, or 2.5 to 3 oz behind a double to fill a Gibraltar. Warmed and barely textured either way: wet, glossy, a skin of foam rather than a layer, well under an eighth of an inch, pouring flat out of the jug"], ["Glass","The 4.5 oz Gibraltar glass, a small straight tumbler with no handle. Some bars use a 4 oz glass cup, and Spanish bars a smaller one again"], ["Serve","Milk to about 130 to 140F, deliberately cooler than the 140 to 150F you steam for a latte, so it goes down in three or four mouthfuls while it is still right. Straight to the guest, no saucer required"], ["Tastes like","Espresso still in front, milk sitting behind it rounding the acid rather than covering it. Sweet, short, and no cap to chew through"], ["Sells to","Mid-afternoon, to the guest who wants milk but not a mouthful of foam, and hard to the specialty regulars who order it by name"]] },
  { dom:"milk", name:"Piccolo", cat:"With Milk", note:"A piccolo is a small latte and a cortado is not, which is the reverse of how most people guess. The piccolo is textured and poured exactly like a latte, foam and pattern and all, just scaled down. The cortado is cut flatter and cooler. Neither has a fixed spec: piccolo is a trade name out of Australian cafes, so ask your own bar what its glass holds.",
    facts:[["Ratio","A single ristretto is the traditional build for a piccolo latte, roughly 9 to 10 g in for about 15 to 20 g out, call it 0.5 to 0.7 oz, in 20 to 25 seconds. Pull that to a full ounce and you have a normale, not a ristretto. Most bars own double baskets only, so a split double or a short double is the working answer, and the range is which basket you have."], ["Volume","3 to 4 oz (90 to 120 mL)"], ["Milk","About 2.5 to 3 oz to fill the glass, steamed to full latte texture: glossy microfoam, a thin cap under about a quarter inch, free-poured so it carries a small pattern. More milk relative to coffee than a cortado, in a smaller glass than a flat white"], ["Glass","A 3 to 4 oz latte glass, straight sided and no handle"], ["Serve","Milk at 140 to 150F into a warmed glass, then out immediately. At this size the surface is large against the liquid under it, so it cools fast and it is a walk rather than a wait on the pass"], ["Tastes like","A latte compressed. Milky and sweet, coffee still readable underneath, finished in four sips"], ["Sells to","To baristas, and to the guest tasting your house espresso with milk before committing to a bigger cup. Also the afternoon order for anyone who has already had two"]] },
  { dom:"milk", name:"Flat White", cat:"With Milk", note:"Flat does not mean no foam. It means no dry cap. A flat white is textured milk all the way down with a thin glossy layer folded through it, and the coffee sits further forward than in a latte simply because the cup is smaller. Be honest that the borders move: the small cup is the one constant, while the ristretto base is a common habit and several large chains define the drink by shot count instead.",
    facts:[["Ratio","A double, 18 to 20 g in, 36 to 40 g out, 25 to 30 seconds, though many New Zealand bars and some Australian ones pull a double ristretto instead for a shorter, sweeter base. Milk fills the rest of a 5 to 6 oz cup, so roughly 1 part espresso to 3 or 4 parts milk depending on the cup and the pull. The range is national habit, not error."], ["Volume","5 to 6 oz (150 to 180 mL)"], ["Milk","About 4 to 4.5 oz, stretched only briefly and then rolled until the jug is smooth and glossy with no bubbles you can see. The foam layer is thin, under about a quarter inch, and folded through the milk rather than sitting on top of it. Paint, not meringue"], ["Glass","Warmed 5 to 6 oz tulip cup with a handle"], ["Serve","Milk at 140 to 150F, poured from height at first so it sinks under the crema and mixes, then close to the surface for the pattern. Filled to the rim and out with the art facing the guest"], ["Tastes like","Coffee forward and sweet, velvety rather than airy, the milk carrying the shot instead of burying it"], ["Sells to","To Australian and New Zealand guests, to specialty regulars, and to anyone who tells you a latte is too milky and a cappuccino too foamy"]] },
  { dom:"milk", name:"Cappuccino", cat:"With Milk", note:"One third espresso, one third milk, one third foam is a teaching phrase, not a measurement, and nobody behind a bar in Italy is measuring it. What actually separates a cappuccino from a flat white at the same volume is air: you stretch longer, start with less milk in the jug, and the drink eats lighter. How thick the cap sits is house tradition. Big dry bubbles and foam separating from the milk are the real fault.",
    facts:[["Ratio","A double, 18 to 20 g in, 36 to 40 g out, 25 to 30 seconds, then milk to fill a 5 to 6 oz cup. The Italian certified spec is built on a single instead, commonly quoted as about 0.8 oz of espresso under about 3.4 oz of milk measured before it is frothed. The range is which tradition your bar follows."], ["Volume","5 to 6 oz (150 to 180 mL)"], ["Milk","About 3 to 3.5 oz in the jug, less than you would start with for a flat white in the same cup, because you stretch this one longer and it gains real volume on the way. Roll it smooth after. The cap runs about a third to a half inch, glossy and wet enough to pour through. Traditional foam sits thicker, specialty foam wetter"], ["Glass","Warmed 5 to 6 oz cup with a handle, on a saucer"], ["Serve","Milk at 140 to 150F, poured to the rim, dusted with cocoa only if the house does that. Out fast, because the cap starts drying the moment the drink stops moving"], ["Tastes like","Sweet and airy, coffee cutting up through the middle, a lighter mouthful than a flat white at the same volume because there is air in it"], ["Sells to","Mornings, and to anyone who wants something to sip slowly. The default order from the guest who does not know what to order"]] },
  { dom:"milk", name:"Latte", cat:"With Milk", note:"Order a latte in Italy and you get a glass of milk; the words you want are caffè latte. Behind the bar, the bigger error is reading latte art as proof of quality. Art tells you the milk was textured well and tells you nothing at all about the shot, and a clean rosetta on a sour, under-extracted double is still a bad drink. Taste the espresso first. Pour second.",
    facts:[["Ratio","A double, 18 to 20 g in, 36 to 40 g out, 25 to 30 seconds, then milk to fill. That works out around 1 part espresso to 5 or more parts milk at 8 oz and up, and nearer 1 to 4 in a 6 oz cup. The range is entirely cup size, which is why a 12 oz latte tastes weaker than an 8 oz one off the identical shot."], ["Volume","8 to 12 oz (240 to 355 mL), and some bars serve 6 oz (180 mL)"], ["Milk","About 6.5 to 10.5 oz depending on the cup, stretched briefly and rolled long until it is glossy and pourable with a thin foam layer around a quarter inch. Less air than a cappuccino, a touch more than a flat white, and smooth enough to hold a pattern"], ["Glass","Warmed 8 to 12 oz cup or a tall glass, handle either way"], ["Serve","Milk at 140 to 150F. Do not push past 160F even when the guest asks for it extra hot, because the sweetness reads lower against the cooked note and the milk starts to smell of it. Poured to the rim, art up"], ["Tastes like","Mild and sweet, milk in front, coffee sitting behind it as a background note. Comfortable rather than intense"], ["Sells to","All day and to everybody, and it is the base under every flavoured drink on your menu"]] },
  { dom:"milk", name:"Mocha", cat:"With Milk", note:"The name is a place, not a flavour. Mokha was the Yemeni port that shipped coffee for centuries, and the beans moving through it carried a cocoa note in the cup, so the word meant coffee long before it meant chocolate. Actual chocolate joined the drink much later. The practical end of it: stir the chocolate into the hot shot before the milk goes in, or it sets in the bottom of the cup.",
    facts:[["Ratio","A double, 18 to 20 g in, 36 to 40 g out, 25 to 30 seconds, then chocolate, then milk to fill. Around 0.75 to 1 oz of sauce is the common house pour, roughly two pumps on most bottles, but sauces differ wildly in sugar and pumps differ in volume, and powders differ more again. Taste yours neat and set the dose against your own cup rather than copying a number."], ["Volume","8 to 12 oz (240 to 355 mL)"], ["Milk","About 6 to 9.5 oz depending on the cup, at latte texture, glossy with a thin foam layer. Run it slightly wetter than a plain latte, because the chocolate brings body of its own and a stiff cap sitting on sauce eats like pudding"], ["Glass","Warmed 8 to 12 oz cup or a tall glass; clear glass if the house builds it in visible layers"], ["Serve","Milk at 140 to 150F, same ceiling as a latte. Whipped cream and a dusting go on last and only if the house does them. Out quickly, because chocolate sinks"], ["Tastes like","Sweet and rich, cocoa in front, coffee arriving underneath it, and a finish that depends entirely on how bitter your chocolate is"], ["Sells to","To the guest who does not really like coffee, to the late table that wants dessert in a cup, and to most of the room at eleven at night"]] },

  /* Brew methods and the leaf. The three fact rows carry different
     questions for the two families, because a brew method is answered by
     ratio, water and time while a tea is answered by leaf, steep heat and
     steep time, and forcing one set of labels on both would make half of
     them lie. */
  { dom:"filter", name:"Pour-over", cat:"Brew Method", note:"Guests and new staff read the bloom as theatre, a pause to let the aroma rise. It is degassing. Fresh coffee is full of carbon dioxide, and gas pushes water away, so a bed that has not released it brews in channels and comes out thin and sour. Wet two to three times the coffee's weight, wait 30 to 45 seconds, then pour. Stale coffee barely blooms, which tells you something.",
    facts:[["Ratio & grind","1:16 to 1:17, so about 59 to 63 g per litre, ground medium, about the coarseness of sand. A cone's single wide opening leaves the coffee bed to set the flow, while a flat bottom's small holes set it themselves, so the same grind will not give you the same time in both"], ["Water","195 to 205F. Use a thermometer rather than a count off the boil, because how fast a kettle falls into that band depends on the kettle, the volume and the room"], ["Time","2:30 to 3:30 for a single cup, 4:00 to 5:00 in a large glass carafe brewer, timed from the first water onto the bed with the bloom counted in"], ["Glass","Pre-heated ceramic cup, or brewed into a carafe and poured, 6 to 10 oz"], ["Tastes like","Clean and light in body, because the paper holds back the oils and the fines, so acidity and aroma sit forward and the finish is short and bright"], ["Sells to","The guest who asks what the coffee is, and the one who wants a cup and not a cocktail. Sells after dessert, and to the table that is not drinking"]] },
  { dom:"filter", name:"French Press", cat:"Brew Method", note:"The press is not a carafe. Plunging does not stop anything; the grounds sit in the water and keep extracting, so the second cup off a table press is bitter and the guest blames the roast. Plunge when the steep is done, pour the lot out, hold the rest in a warmed vessel. Press gently too, because forcing the screen drives fines through it.",
    facts:[["Ratio & grind","1:15 to 1:16, so about 63 to 67 g per litre, ground coarse like rough sea salt. Known as the cafetiere or the press pot, and it is a full immersion brewer with a metal screen, not a paper filter"], ["Water","200 to 205F, the top of the standard band, because an immersion loses heat into the vessel and across the whole steep"], ["Time","4 to 5 minutes of steep in a pre-heated press, then plunge slowly and decant the whole thing straight away"], ["Glass","Decanted into a pre-heated mug or cup, 6 to 8 oz. The press is a brewer, not a serving vessel"], ["Tastes like","Heavy and round, full of oil and fine sediment, low clarity, long finish. The same coffee reads a shade darker here than it does through paper"], ["Sells to","The guest who says filter coffee is weak. Good for two at a table, good after a heavy meal, and good when the machine is down"]] },
  { dom:"filter", name:"AeroPress", cat:"Brew Method", note:"It is sold as espresso and it is not. Your arm makes well under one bar of pressure; an espresso machine works at about nine. What comes out is a concentrate, not a shot, so it carries no real crema and will not hold a milk pattern. Build a strong black coffee on it and sell that honestly. It is the best brewer you can keep in a drawer.",
    facts:[["Ratio & grind","1:14 to 1:16 for a cup you drink as it comes, or about 1:8 as a concentrate you lengthen with hot water. Medium-fine, finer than drip and coarser than espresso. Standard and inverted are the two ways to build it"], ["Water","175 to 205F, and lighter roasts want the top of that band while darker roasts get harsh there"], ["Time","1:00 to 2:30 start to cup, including a slow 20 to 30 second press"], ["Glass","Pressed straight into a pre-heated cup or mug, 4 to 8 oz, or into a carafe and lengthened"], ["Tastes like","Clean like a paper filter cup but thicker and sweeter, low in perceived acidity, short finish, and unusually forgiving of a rough grinder"], ["Sells to","One cup, fast, at a bar with no brewer. The single after-dinner coffee, the staff cup at shift change, and the outpost service bar"]] },
  { dom:"filter", name:"Batch Brew", cat:"Brew Method", note:"Hot is not fresh. The plate keeps cooking what is already brewed, so a pot held thirty minutes on a burner goes flat and bitter though it was fine when it landed; brew into an airpot instead. And a half batch in a full-size brewer is not half a pot, it is a shallow bed the water runs straight through. Brew the size the basket wants.",
    facts:[["Ratio & grind","About 55 to 65 g per litre, so roughly 1:15 to 1:18, ground medium like sand, and ground differently for a flat basket than for a cone. Trade names are auto-drip, bulk brew, or just the pot"], ["Water","195 to 205F in the brew basket, which is not the same thing as what the machine's display claims. Check it with a probe in the slurry rather than trusting the readout"], ["Time","Roughly 4 to 8 minutes of brew cycle for a full batch and less for a small one, then a 30 to 60 minute window in a sealed airpot in which it is still worth selling, far shorter than that on an open burner"], ["Glass","Airpot or insulated carafe, poured into a pre-heated mug, 6 to 12 oz"], ["Tastes like","Even and deliberately middle of the road, medium body, no single note forward, which is exactly what makes it the coffee for everybody"], ["Sells to","Brunch, banquet, the check-back, and the whole table at once. It is the only coffee you can pour forty of without stopping"]] },
  { dom:"filter", name:"Cold Brew", cat:"Brew Method", note:"Smooth is not weak, and it is not low acid either. Measured pH sits close to hot brewed coffee; what cold water leaves behind is a set of sour and bitter compounds, not acid as a number. A concentrate poured straight over ice is a heavy dose of caffeine whatever it tastes like. Cut it, date it, keep it cold, because coffee counts as a low-acid food, which is a safety class and not a flavour claim.",
    facts:[["Ratio & grind","1:8 as a concentrate you cut one to one at service, or to taste, or about 1:15 brewed ready to drink. Coarse, at least as coarse as a press. Full immersion is mizudashi; the slow drip tower is Kyoto style"], ["Water","Never heated. Room temperature near 68F, or refrigerated near 38F, which runs slower and reads cleaner. Some health codes want the whole steep refrigerated, so check yours before you set a bucket on the counter overnight"], ["Time","12 to 24 hours of steep, then filter. Keep it cold, date it, and work through it inside about a week"], ["Glass","Tall glass over ice, or a rocks with one large cube, 8 to 12 oz"], ["Tastes like","Round and low in perceived sourness, cocoa and dried fruit, heavy in body off an immersion and cleaner off a drip, very long"], ["Sells to","The iced coffee guest all summer, and the base for anything shaken. It is also the cheapest way to put good coffee into a cocktail"]] },
  { dom:"filter", name:"Nitro Cold Brew", cat:"Brew Method", note:"Nitrogen adds no flavour and no sweetness at all. It is barely soluble in water, so unlike carbon dioxide it forms no carbonic acid and gives no prickle; what it does is break out through the faucet's restrictor plate as very fine bubbles, and that cascade and dense head are what the tongue reads as creamy. This is usually straight nitrogen, not the blend the beer side calls beer gas.",
    facts:[["Ratio & grind","The same cold brew base, coarse, but brewed nearer 1:15 to be poured straight, because nothing gets diluted in the glass and there is no ice to hide behind"], ["Water","Never heated. Brewed cold, then held and poured at 34 to 38F, because warm product breaks gas out of solution in the line and pours as foam"], ["Time","The 12 to 24 hour steep, then charged and rested cold, then a 30 to 60 second cascade in the glass before the head sets"], ["Glass","Straight pint or a footed tulip, no ice and no straw, 10 to 12 oz. Set it down and let it settle in front of the guest"], ["Tastes like","Reads sweeter and creamier than the same coffee poured flat, without being sweeter. Dense fine head, soft round mouthfeel, none of the prickle of carbonation"], ["Sells to","The guest who wants a beer and cannot have one. Sells all afternoon, sells to the designated driver, and sells itself across the room because it looks like a stout"]] },
  { dom:"leaf", name:"Black Tea", cat:"Tea", note:"When strong black tea chills it goes cloudy. That is tea cream, caffeine binding with the tea's polyphenols as the temperature falls, and it says the tea was strong, not that it spoiled. Stirring will not clear it; gentle reheating will, and that is your proof for a doubting guest. Brew it double strength and chill it fast over fresh ice, which keeps the haze fine and keeps a hot pot from sitting out.",
    facts:[["Leaf","Camellia sinensis, fully oxidized rather than fermented. Hongcha in Chinese, red tea, named for the colour of the liquor and not the leaf, and not the rooibos an English-speaking guest may mean by the same two words. Orthodox whole leaf for the pot, or CTC, crush tear curl, for the bag and for anything that has to take milk"], ["Steep","205 to 212F, freshly boiled. Black takes a full boil, and so do pu-erh and a dark roasted oolong; it is green and white leaf that want cooler water. Delicate first flush and fine small-leaf Chinese blacks are better nearer 195F"], ["Time","3 to 5 minutes Western style, and that is your one good steep in a pot. Gongfu style at a high leaf load and short infusions will give you four or five"], ["Glass","Pre-heated pot into a cup or mug, 6 to 8 oz. Iced, a Collins over fresh ice"], ["Tastes like","Malt, dried fruit and cocoa, a drying astringency down the sides of the tongue, and enough backbone to take milk and sugar without falling apart"], ["Sells to","Breakfast service, the guest who is not drinking, and the whole iced tea programme. It is the base under half your zero proof list"]] },
  { dom:"leaf", name:"Green Tea", cat:"Tea", note:"Boiling water does not burn the leaf, it extracts too fast. Catechins and caffeine, the bitter drying part, come out quickest at high heat, while the sweet savoury amino acids dissolve readily even in cool water, so the cooler shorter steep you were taught is a change of balance and not a weaker cup. The other wrong belief is that green tea is raw. Heat is what keeps it green.",
    facts:[["Leaf","Camellia sinensis, unoxidized but nowhere near unprocessed. The kill-green step, shaqing in China and sassei in Japan, heats the leaf to kill the enzyme that would otherwise oxidize it: steamed in Japan, pan-fired in China, and that one choice sets everything downstream. Japanese leaf is commonly fixed the same day it is picked, while Chinese leaf is often withered first"], ["Steep","160 to 180F, and the more shaded the leaf the lower you go. Gyokuro goes lower still, near 120 to 140F"], ["Time","1 to 2 minutes Western style, or about 60 seconds for sencha in a kyusu. It re-steeps two or three times, and the second pour is the short one because the leaf is already open, with the third stretched back out again"], ["Glass","Small pot or kyusu into a small cup, 4 to 6 oz. Warm the vessel, then let the water fall in temperature in a cooling bowl, the yuzamashi, or in the cups, so the leaf meets the water you actually meant. Glass if the leaf is worth looking at"], ["Tastes like","Grassy and marine if it is Japanese, nutty and toasted if it is Chinese. Light body, real savoury weight, and bitter the second you overdo it"], ["Sells to","After sushi, after anything fried, and to the guest who wants something and nothing. Also the base for a cold tea highball, brewed that day and kept cold, because brewed tea is a food and not a shelf item"]] },
  { dom:"leaf", name:"White Tea", cat:"Tea", note:"Delicate is the wrong word and it costs you money. Those buds are dense and slow to give anything up, so the cool water and short steep the charts call for underextract them and the guest pays your top price for hot water. Give it heat and give it time. And do not sell it as the low caffeine option: the bud carries the highest caffeine concentration on the plant.",
    facts:[["Leaf","Camellia sinensis, withered and dried and almost nothing else. Baicha. Lightly oxidized rather than unoxidized, because there is no kill-green step to arrest it early. Bai Hao Yinzhen, Silver Needle, is buds only; Bai Mudan, White Peony, is a bud with two leaves"], ["Steep","175 to 195F, and a bud-heavy tea takes the top of that range. That is hotter than most printed charts, and the disagreement is real, so the card takes the specialist side on purpose rather than pretending it is settled"], ["Time","3 to 5 minutes Western style, longer than most people expect. Re-steeps three or four times and often opens up on the second"], ["Glass","Glass pot or a tall glass so the buds show, warmed first, into a small cup, 4 to 6 oz"], ["Tastes like","Hay, melon and honey, almost no astringency, thin on arrival and then surprisingly long. Aged white goes toward dried apricot and warm spice"], ["Sells to","Your most expensive tea by the pot, and it sells on being looked at. The guest who says they do not like tea, and the guest who wants the list's high end"]] },
  { dom:"leaf", name:"Oolong", cat:"Tea", note:"Guests read oolong as one dial running from green to black, and the number on the tin still will not tell you what is in the cup. Roast runs on its own axis: a lightly oxidized ball rolled leaf taken through heavy charcoal drinks dark, and a highly oxidized strip left unroasted drinks bright. Ask how it was fired, not just how far it was oxidized. And stop throwing the leaf out after one steep.",
    facts:[["Leaf","Camellia sinensis, partly oxidized, commonly published as roughly 10 to 80 percent with both edges argued over. Wulong, or qingcha. Shape follows the region more than the oxidation: ball rolled in Anxi and much of Taiwan, twisted into strips in Wuyi and Phoenix. Roast is a separate axis from oxidation and matters just as much"], ["Steep","195 to 212F, and the darker and more heavily roasted it is, the hotter it wants"], ["Time","Gongfu style, a quick rinse then infusions of 20 to 45 seconds, stretched longer as the leaf tires, six to eight of them off one leaf. Western style, 2 to 3 minutes, twice"], ["Glass","Small gaiwan or clay pot into thimble cups, 1 to 2 oz a pour. A mug if the room is not that kind of room"], ["Tastes like","At the green end, orchid and cream and butter. At the dark end, roasted nut, stone fruit and caramel. At both ends, texture is the whole point"], ["Sells to","The tea guest who already knows tea, and the table that will sit for an hour. The single best tea to pour in stages at a bar"]] },
  { dom:"leaf", name:"Pu-erh", cat:"Tea", note:"Fermented here does not mean oxidized. Ripe pu-erh is wet piled, wo dui, under heat and damp for weeks while moulds and bacteria work the leaf, which is closer to what koji does to rice than to anything that happens in a black tea. The second confusion costs more: raw is the one that transforms with age, while ripe arrives finished and settles rather than changes. Do not pay aged money for ripe.",
    facts:[["Leaf","Camellia sinensis var. assamica from Yunnan, post-fermented rather than oxidized. Hou fajiao. Sheng is raw and changes over years or decades; shou is ripe, wet piled for weeks to reach something in that direction inside a single season. Usually pressed into cakes, also bricks, nests and loose"], ["Steep","205 to 212F, a full boil, after a rinse. Young raw is the one to pull back from, nearer 195 to 205F, because full heat drives its bitterness hard"], ["Time","Rinse 5 to 10 seconds and throw that away, then infusions of 10 to 30 seconds, stretched longer as the session goes. Ten or more steeps off one piece of cake is ordinary, not a stunt"], ["Glass","Gaiwan or a small clay pot, warmed first, into thimble cups, 1 to 2 oz a pour"], ["Tastes like","Ripe: wet earth, damp wood and cocoa, thick and soft and sweet with almost no astringency. Young raw: bitter and astringent, vegetal, apricot, alive, and smoky in some. Aged raw is where the two meet"], ["Sells to","After a big meal, because it drinks like a digestif. Sells to the whisky drinker, and to anyone who sat through the koji lesson"]] },
  { dom:"leaf", name:"Matcha", cat:"Tea", note:"Ceremonial grade is a marketing phrase with nobody behind it. It guarantees nothing, so taste before you buy. What decides quality is the leaf and the grind: only shaded tencha, destemmed and stone ground fine enough to suspend rather than sink, is matcha, and powdered sencha is a different product sold under the same word. Dull yellow green means old or cheap, whatever the tin says.",
    facts:[["Leaf","Tencha: Camellia sinensis shaded for roughly three to four weeks before picking, steamed, then dried without rolling, destemmed and deveined, and finally stone ground. Unoxidized. The unrolled leaf is what separates tencha from sencha, and you are drinking the leaf itself rather than an infusion of it, which is why every rule below is different"], ["Steep","165 to 175F for usucha, thin tea. Koicha, thick tea, takes water at about the same temperature but roughly twice the powder into half the water or less, which is why it lands several times the concentration rather than several times the dose"], ["Time","No steep at all. Sift, then whisk usucha 15 to 30 seconds with a chasen. Koicha is not whisked to a foam, it is kneaded slowly into a smooth paste, and at that strength it wants your best powder. Nothing re-steeps, because nothing is left behind to steep"], ["Glass","Chawan, the tea bowl, 2 to 3 oz for usucha. For bar service, a latte cup or a tall glass over ice"], ["Tastes like","Sweet grass and seaweed over a savoury weight, thick stable foam, and a bitterness that arrives late and is meant to be there"], ["Sells to","The guest who is off coffee but not off caffeine. Lattes, lemonade, and the dessert pairing that does not involve alcohol"]] },
  { dom:"leaf", name:"Herbal Tisane", cat:"Tea", note:"Herbal does not mean caffeine free. Yerba mate and guayusa brew like tea and carry caffeine, guarana in a blend carries the same compound, and the cup can land above the tea the guest was avoiding, so read the tin before eleven at night. Herbal does not mean inert either. Some of these are cautioned against in pregnancy or alongside medication, so name what is in it and leave the decision with the guest.",
    facts:[["Leaf","No Camellia sinensis in it at all, so the oxidation ladder that sorts the rest of your tea list does not apply, though rooibos itself is oxidized to go red and green rooibos is the same plant left unoxidized. Tisane is the accurate word. Leaves and flowers infuse; roots, bark and seed want a decoction, which means an actual simmer rather than a steep"], ["Steep","208 to 212F, a full boil, for very nearly all of it"], ["Time","5 to 10 minutes infused, or 10 to 20 minutes simmered for roots and bark. Most give everything they have in one pass and do not come back for a second"], ["Glass","Glass pot or a glass mug, warmed first because it is taking boiling water, 8 to 10 oz, because half of these are worth looking at"], ["Tastes like","Whatever it is made of. Chamomile is apple and honey, peppermint is cold and sharp, hibiscus is cranberry-sour and will redden anything it touches, rooibos is sweet wood"], ["Sells to","Last call, the designated driver, the guest who is done drinking but not done sitting, and anyone who wants a warm thing at the end of the night. Hibiscus doubles as your house zero proof base"]] },
  { dom:"leaf", name:"Masala Chai", cat:"Tea", note:"You do not steep this and pour milk on it. Leaf, water and cracked whole spice go on the heat together and the milk boils in the same pot, and that boil is what pulls tannin and spice oil at once, which is why a delicate leaf would vanish in it. Sugar goes in the pot, not the cup. A pump of syrup into steamed milk is a different drink, so call it what it is.",
    facts:[["Leaf","Strong CTC Assam, fully oxidized, deliberately the cheap robust grade, because it has to survive milk, sugar and a hard boil. Masala is the spice and chai is simply the word for tea, so chai tea says tea twice"], ["Steep","212F, held at a boil. This is not a steep, it is a decoction"], ["Time","5 to 10 minutes in total: spice and leaf into the water, then the milk into the same pot, brought back up and held. One use only, the leaf comes out spent. Batched, it stops being tea and becomes a dairy product on your line, so hold it hot, above 135F, or chill it and refrigerate it, and never let it coast warm through a shift"], ["Glass","Small glass tumbler, the cutting chai glass, warmed first because it is taking boiling milk, 4 to 5 oz, or a clay kulhad. A mug if you must"], ["Tastes like","Sweet, thick and spiced, the tannin of a strong black cut by milk fat and sugar, warming rather than sharp, with the spice sitting in the finish"], ["Sells to","All winter and all afternoon, and to everyone who is sure they do not like tea. It is also the base for the chai toddy already on your list, and for the spiked build when you want one"]] },

  /* The fault board. A bartender with a bad cup in their hand reads this
     one rather than hunting through the lessons, which is why every card
     says what it READS AS before it says what causes it, and why every one
     names where the same thing is correct rather than a fault. */
  { dom:"faults", name:"Over-extracted", cat:"Coffee", note:"The wrong belief is that bitter means strong, so you cut the dose or shorten the shot to settle it down. Strength is how much coffee is in the water. Extraction is how much you pulled out of the grounds. They move separately, and an over-extracted shot is often weak and bitter at once, which is why cutting the dose without coarsening the grind usually makes it worse.",
    facts:[["Reads as","Harsh and ashy, with a drying grip that takes the whole mouth and hangs on after the swallow. The bitterness is flat rather than interesting, and it arrives at the back end of the sip instead of lifting off the top. The shot usually ran slow, went pale at the tail and kept running, and what landed in the cup looks dark but drinks thin. Take the guest's words carefully here, because two different faults share them. Ashtray, ash and scorched wood off a shot you just pulled point at your extraction. Bottom of the pot, stewed, or left on a burner almost always means brewed coffee held hot too long, which is a holding fault rather than an extraction one and will not answer to anything you do at the grinder. Ask what they were drinking and how long ago it was made before you touch a dial."], ["Comes from","Too much came out of the grounds, not too much time passed. Extraction is how much of the bed you dissolve, and it rises when you grind finer, run more water through for the same dose, or brew hotter. The working order the trade teaches is that salts and acids leave first, sweetness follows, and the drying bitter compounds come last, so pushing past the sweet part lands you in the tail. Treat that as a useful map rather than as chemistry, because the compounds overlap far more than the ladder suggests. Two causes break the slow shot picture and catch people out. A dose too light for the basket gives each gram more water than the recipe intended, so the shot gushes and still over-extracts. A channel does both at once, scouring the path it found while leaving the rest of the bed behind, which is why a channelled cup can read sour and bitter in the same sip. And a dark or scorched roast tastes ashy however well you pull it."], ["Correct in","Bitterness as structure is correct. Bitterness as the entire drink is not. Milk brings sugar and fat that mute coffee, so a shot for a cappuccino or a mocha is built to be tasted through something, and a darker roast is often chosen for exactly that weight. How houses get there is genuinely contested, so do not carry one shop's answer into another. One tradition goes short and concentrated for milk, on the argument that strength is what survives the pour. The other runs a longer ratio and accepts more of the tail, on the argument that it gives the drink something to push against. Both are in daily use and both can be right in the cup. What neither tradition wants is the ashy, drying finish this card describes, so judge the milk drink by tasting the finished thing rather than by which camp the recipe came from."], ["Your move","Dump it, and do not sweeten your way out. Then measure before you adjust, because the fastest way to chase your tail is to move the grinder on a fault the grinder did not cause. Weigh the dose going in and the drink coming out rather than trusting the button, and confirm the basket is the size that dose was written for. Watch the next shot leave the basket, and if it gushed or sprayed, fix the puck first, since coarsening a channelled shot only makes a bigger channel. If the numbers and the flow are honest and it still bites, coarsen one step and pull again, changing one thing only so you can read the result. Taste the new shot on its own before it goes near milk. If every bag from that roaster does the same thing at every grind setting, stop dialling and talk to whoever buys the coffee."]] },
  { dom:"faults", name:"Under-extracted", cat:"Coffee", note:"The wrong belief is that sour and bitter sit at two ends of one dial, so a sour shot can be walked toward sweet by letting it run longer. Run a coarse, fast, channelled shot longer and you add the bitter tail on top of the sour front and serve both at once. Fix the puck and the grind, not the stopwatch.",
    facts:[["Reads as","Sour and sharp, and the guest reaches for sugar before they say anything. It reads as raw lemon or unripe fruit, with a salty edge and a hollow middle, and it is gone a second after the swallow instead of finishing sweet. The shot usually ran fast and pale. Crema comes up thin and blond, but do not hang the diagnosis on that, because thin crema also turns up on coffee that is simply old, on decaf, and on a roast that had nothing much to give, and none of those are fixed the same way. The taste is the reliable half. Sour and bright and sharp is extraction. Flat, dull and papery with no sourness in it at all is age, and that is a different card."], ["Comes from","Too little came out of the bed. The sharp, salty front of the run comes off first and the sweetness sits behind it, so anything that cuts short how much you actually dissolve strands you at the front. Grind too coarse and the water finds too little surface. Cut the yield short and you stop before the sweet part arrives. Brew below temperature and everything slows, which on a bar is far more often a machine that has not recovered between drinks or a portafilter that has been sitting out cold than a setting somebody changed. One cause runs against the fast shot picture: a dose too heavy for the basket slows the flow down while still giving each gram less water than the recipe wanted, so it drags and lands thick and sour at once, concentrated without being extracted. And a channel leaves most of the bed barely wetted while scouring one path, which gives you sour and bitter in the same mouthful."], ["Correct in","Acidity is correct, and it is most of what a good light roast is for, so be careful with the word before you use it in front of the guest who ordered that coffee on purpose. Brightness you would want more of is one thing. A cup you want to put sugar in is another. The ristretto is where the trade genuinely splits. Everyone agrees it is a deliberately short ratio. One camp holds that a shorter ratio is by definition a lower yield and therefore the same thing as under-extraction, and the other holds that a ristretto ground and dosed for the job lands sweet and complete on its own terms. Do not settle that at the bar. Pull it the way the house pulls it, taste it, and ask whether it finishes sweet or simply stops."], ["Your move","Look before you grind. Pull one and watch it leave: a spray or a jet to one side on a bottomless basket, or two spouts running at different rates, is a puck problem, and grinding finer on a channelled puck only feeds the channel. If that is what you see, re-dose, level the bed, tamp flat and pull again before you touch the grinder. Check the cheap things next, because they cost you nothing: the portafilter has been in the group and is hot, the basket is clean and clear, the machine has had a moment to recover after the last run of drinks. Then, and only then, grind one step finer, pull, and taste. Change one thing at a time. If it turns sweet, you found it. If it goes sour and harsh together instead, you did not fix a channel, you made it bigger."]] },
  { dom:"faults", name:"Stale", cat:"Coffee", note:"The wrong belief is that a shiny, oily bean is a fresh one. That sheen mostly tells you the roast went dark, and age pushes oil out too, so it never tells you the coffee is fresh. Oil on the surface has nowhere to hide from the air. The other half of the belief is that you can dose your way out. A bigger dose of stale coffee is just more cardboard.",
    facts:[["Reads as","Nothing much. Not offensive, just absent: flat and papery, a cardboard or old wood note where the aroma should be, and a cup that smells of almost nothing when it lands. Crema comes up thin and falls apart before the drink reaches the table, though thin crema on its own proves little, since an under-extracted shot and a decaf do the same. The separator is what is missing rather than what is wrong. Stale coffee is dull and short with no sharp edge to it, where an under-extracted shot is actively sour. Guests call it dusty, say it tastes old or watery, or ask whether you gave them decaf by mistake."], ["Comes from","Time and air taking back what the roast put in. Roasting loads the bean with carbon dioxide and with volatile aromatics, the gas carries that aroma up into your nose as the drink is poured, and both start leaving the moment the roast ends. Grinding is the accelerator, because it opens an enormous amount of new surface at once, which is why pre-ground coffee sitting in a doser or a tub goes first, faster than anything else on the bar. Beans left in an open hopper are the slower version of the same thing, helped along by grinder heat, light and damp. Keep this apart from the card next to it. Staling is loss, the aroma leaving a bean that had it, and it gives you an empty cup. When the bean's own fats oxidise you get something new and actively foul instead, which is rancidity rather than staleness."], ["Correct in","Age is correct in places. Green coffee held on purpose, as in monsooned and aged styles, comes out low in acid and deliberately woody, and that is a choice a buyer made rather than a bag going off. A just roasted bag also genuinely drinks better after a rest, because the coffee needs to give up some of its gas before it will pull evenly, and a bag opened too early can fizz and run uneven on you. Take the length of that rest from the bag and from the freshness lesson already in The Shot rather than from a rule of thumb. Those are age doing something. Staling is a bag or a hopper losing what it already had, and that is correct nowhere."], ["Your move","Find the roast date before you touch a dial, on the bag and on whatever is in the hopper, and find out when that bag was opened. Then use your nose rather than the machine. Grind a fresh dose and smell it at the grinder: if the aroma shows up for a second and dies, and the cup that follows smells of nothing, you have your answer. The decisive test is to run the next drink on a freshly opened bag, because staleness is the fault that a bean change fixes. If it comes back alive, the machine was innocent. If nothing changes, stop blaming the coffee and go and smell your equipment. Then keep only what the shift will use in the hopper, close the bag properly, rotate stock so the oldest gets used first, and grind to order rather than ahead."]] },
  { dom:"faults", name:"Rancid oils", cat:"Coffee", note:"The wrong belief is that a hot water flush counts as cleaning. Water does not dissolve oxidised fat, which is exactly why detergent exists. Flushing the group moves loose grounds and nothing else, and a dark metal part can be thick with rancid oil and still look spotless under bar lighting. Judge by smell and by what comes off on a white cloth, never by appearance.",
    facts:[["Reads as","Actively foul rather than merely dull. A greasy, fishy or crayon note off the cup, something like old fryer oil or wet paint, and a film that coats the roof of the mouth and stays long after the drink has gone. What marks it out is that it does not belong to one drink. Every cup off that machine carries it, the next bag of beans does not lift it, moving the grind does not lift it either, and it gets worse with time rather than fading. Use one split to place it before you start pulling things apart. If black coffee and espresso carry it too, the fault is in the brew path. If only the milk drinks carry it, look at the steam wand, the pitchers and the milk itself first, because dairy fat goes off the same way and smells much the same."], ["Comes from","Coffee carries fat, and the fat it leaves behind on your equipment oxidises and turns rancid. It collects where you cannot see it and where heat and air both reach it: the shower screen and the group gasket, the holes in the basket, the underside of the spouts, the grinder chute and the burr chamber, a brewer's spray head, an urn spout, the inside of a thermal carafe. Because this is fat breaking down rather than aroma leaving, it behaves the opposite way to staleness. It does not fade. It builds, and it transfers into everything that passes through, which is why a clean fresh bag tastes exactly as bad as the last one. An old group gasket is the usual holdout, because it is soft, it is porous, and it has been absorbing this for however long it has been in there."], ["Correct in","Nowhere. There is no drink and no style in which oxidised coffee fat is wanted, no roast level and no grind that makes it read as character, and nothing you can do at the grinder that turns it into anything else. Draw one line before you condemn a cup, though. Coffee oil itself is wanted, and an unfiltered brew such as a press pot is heavier and oilier on purpose, so a thick mouthfeel is not this fault. Rancid is not richness. It is the smell of paint and old fryer oil sitting on top of the drink, and it turns up whether the brew is heavy or light. If a cup smells of it, something in the path has not been cleaned, and that is the whole diagnosis rather than the start of one."], ["Your move","Follow the path with your nose before you follow it with a tool. Smell the empty portafilter, the grinder chute and a dry carafe rather than the coffee. Pull the basket and hold it up to the light to see whether the holes are actually clear, then wipe the screen with a white cloth and look at what colour comes off. Split the cleaning by part, because one method does not cover all of it. The brew path takes a proper machine detergent, and a backflush where the machine is built to take one, always to the manufacturer's instructions, then enough clean water afterwards that nothing tastes of soap, and the first drinks after it go down the drain rather than to a guest. The grinder is cleaned dry, with a brush, a vacuum and cleaning tablets, never with water, which rusts burrs and cakes the chute. Put a clean basket and a clean carafe in the path meanwhile, and if the smell survives a proper clean, change the group gasket and the screen."]] },
  { dom:"faults", name:"Scale and water", cat:"Coffee", note:"The wrong belief is that a filter means you cannot get scale. Carbon takes out chlorine and does nothing to hardness. A scale inhibitor cartridge only stops the mineral depositing and leaves it in the cup. Sodium softening removes hardness and leaves the bicarbonate, so the machine survives and the coffee stays flat. And cartridges are rated by volume, so a busy bar exhausts one months early.",
    facts:[["Reads as","Flat and chalky. The coffee reads muted and dusty, the brightness is not replaced by anything but simply absent, and the guest reaches for watery or thin rather than dirty. Separate that from a tap note, because they are different faults with different cartridges behind them: swimming pool, plastic or wet cardboard is chlorine and belongs to your carbon stage, while an absence with nothing in its place is the alkalinity. The louder tells sit outside the cup. White crust around the group and the hot water tap, a ring inside the kettle or urn, a furred element, slow flow, weak steam, and a shot temperature that will not sit still from one drink to the next. Test the crust before you trust it, because milk residue wipes off with a damp cloth and scale does not."], ["Comes from","Two different things in the water, and the trade calls both of them hard. Hardness is the calcium and the magnesium. Alkalinity is the bicarbonate, and alkalinity is the half that costs you the cup, because bicarbonate buffers the coffee's own acids and neutralises the brightness before it ever reaches the guest, leaving something chalky and hollow in its place. The machine is taken by the other half, and only by the part of it carried as bicarbonate: heat drives carbon dioxide out of the water, calcium carbonate dissolves worse hot than cold and falls out of solution, and it plates onto the element, the boiler wall and the flow path. There it insulates the element from the water, so the element runs hotter while the water gets less of that heat, recovery slows, the probe reads late, and the number on the display stops describing the water arriving at the basket. The same deposit narrows the flow path and chokes the steam. Which is why a bar can soften its water, stop the scale dead, and still serve a flat cup."], ["Correct in","Mineral in the water is correct and necessary, and so is a little bicarbonate with it. Calcium and magnesium help carry flavour out of the grounds, and a small amount of buffering keeps the cup from reading sharp and thin. Water stripped bare is the opposite fault rather than the safe end of the scale: it tastes hollow, it is aggressive to copper and brass, and on a machine that senses its boiler level by conductivity it can leave the autofill unable to read its own water. Mineral in the water is a tool. Mineral on the element is the fault."], ["Your move","Taste the water on its own before you blame the coffee, and let that taste decide whether you are chasing chlorine or alkalinity, because they are not the same cartridge. Look at the group, the hot water tap and the kettle element for crust, and wipe it to see whether you are looking at scale or at milk. Then check the date and the volume counter on the filter cartridge, because that is usually the answer and usually nobody's job. Report the rest rather than fixing it, since weak steam, wandering temperature and slow flow are a service call. Descaling itself is contested and the only ruling that matters is your own machine's: a plumbed commercial machine is descaled by a technician, some countertop machines ship with a descale cycle and a branded product that the manual requires, and improvised vinegar belongs in neither."]] },
  { dom:"faults", name:"Scalded milk", cat:"Milk & Tea", note:"The wrong belief is that extra hot is simply more of the same, so you hold the wand in longer for the guest who asks for it. Past the ceiling the milk stops gaining and starts losing: the sweetness goes, the sulphur note arrives, and the foam will not survive the walk to the table. Serve extra hot by warming the cup, shortening the path and saying where the ceiling is.",
    facts:[["Reads as","Cooked. It tastes like the skin on a pan of hot milk or like tinned evaporated milk, the sweetness has gone, and a flat scorched edge sits behind it. Some guests read it as eggy or faintly sulphurous, and that word is the one that settles it, because a drink that is merely badly textured never smells of sulphur. The foam is coarse and already draining, the pitcher was too hot to keep a hand on, and the drink goes down on the table to cool and never gets picked back up."], ["Comes from","Heat taken past the point of no return, and the flavour and the texture fail alongside each other for separate reasons. Warming makes milk read sweeter up to a window, and past that window the whey proteins unfold and expose sulphur groups they had kept folded away, which is the cooked flavour and the eggy smell you are meeting. The foam goes at the same moment but not for that reason: heat thins the liquid, the fat in it is fully melted, and the films between the bubbles drain and merge faster than they can hold, so the foam coarsens and sets into a dry cap while you are still pouring. Do not tell yourself that denatured protein cannot hold air, because ultra heat treated milk is denatured before it ever reaches you and it foams, and in much of the world it is what the latte is made from. What kills this pitcher is the temperature in it right now, not its history. And milk keeps climbing after the wand comes out, so a pitcher you stopped at the ceiling went past it in your hand."], ["Correct in","Genuinely correct in several places. Scalding is deliberate in bread doughs, where the heat deactivates a milk protein that would otherwise weaken the gluten, and it is traditional in custards. On your own bar the boiled milk drinks are built on it: masala chai simmered in milk, hot chocolate, and the hot batter drinks on a winter list all want cooked milk flavour and read thin without it. What is wrong here is cooked milk in a drink whose entire point was sweetness, so the fault is decided by which drink you are holding and not by which flavour is in it."], ["Your move","Do not serve it, and do not try to rescue it with a splash of cold milk, because the cooked flavour is already made and all that buys you is more of it at a lower temperature. Start again with fresh cold milk in a cold pitcher, and cut the steam earlier than feels right, since the milk carries on climbing after you stop. Never re-steam a pitcher. Wipe and purge the wand before the next one, and follow your house order on which of the two comes first, because both orders are taught and both defend the tip. Before you call it scalded at all, put a hand on the pitcher and smell it, because a pitcher you can hold and milk that still smells sweet is a texture fault and belongs on the split milk card instead."]] },
  { dom:"faults", name:"Split milk", cat:"Milk & Tea", note:"The wrong belief is that a curdled cup means the milk has turned. Fresh milk curdles on contact with enough acid or alcohol, especially hot, especially skimmed, and especially in a plant milk without the buffering salts a barista version carries. Older milk gives way sooner because it is more acidic, but the mechanism is chemistry rather than spoilage. Smell and taste before you dump a jug.",
    facts:[["Reads as","Two drinks in one cup. A stiff dry cap of big bubbles sitting on thin white liquid, a hole that opens in the middle as you pour, art that will not float, and a guest who gets dry foam first and watery milk second. The pitcher was not too hot to hold and the milk does not smell cooked, and that is what separates this from scalded milk, which is a flavour fault you can smell. In a hot tea or a hot build it shows up as something else entirely: visible flecks and grain, a curdled look at the surface, and a drink that will not come back together however long you stir it."], ["Comes from","Two different failures sharing one word. Foam splits when the milk was stretched but never spun back together, so the air sits as large bubbles that rise while the liquid drains out beneath them, and a pitcher left standing splits faster the longer it waits. Curdling is the casein itself letting go. The micelles stay apart because their surface keeps them apart, and acid, alcohol, heat and the milk's own age each take that away in turn, acid by carrying the milk down towards the point where casein has no charge left to hold it up, alcohol by stripping the water off the micelle surface, age by doing the acid's work slowly. Tannin does not belong on that list in the way the trade puts it there. Tannin binds milk protein, which is exactly why milk softens a hard tea, but a strong tea on its own rarely throws curd. What throws curd in a tea build is acid, heat and old milk, and the tea is usually supplying only the heat."], ["Correct in","Curdling is a technique and your own list already uses it. Clarified milk punch works by splitting milk on purpose with citrus and then filtering the drink back through the curd, which is how it comes out clear and how it keeps, and the same split is how fresh cheeses are made. Note that it pours the opposite way to the rule below and that nothing is contradictory about it, because the punch carries enough acid to take the whole batch over whichever way you pour. Split foam in a pitcher is correct nowhere. A guest ordering a dry cappuccino is asking for more foam, not for separated milk, and handing them a split pitcher is still the fault."], ["Your move","For foam, pour it or lose it. Keep the pitcher moving while you wait, pour inside the window, and know the limit of that swirl, which brings back a pitcher that has only begun to separate and does nothing at all once there is a dry cap over thin milk. Past that, start again with fresh cold milk rather than putting a dead jug back on the wand. For a curdled build, smell and date the milk first, then control how the two meet: pour the hot liquid into the milk rather than running a thin stream of milk into hot acid, so the milk stays in the majority the whole way instead of meeting the worst of it drop by drop. Reach for whole milk where you can. Whether milk or tea goes into the cup first is an argument the trade will not settle, so settle it here on this ground alone."]] },
  { dom:"faults", name:"Stewed tea", cat:"Milk & Tea", note:"The wrong belief is that a top up fixes it. Water does dilute the grip, so the guest is not wrong that it helps, but it dilutes the flavour in step and cannot touch the aromatics you drove off, so you hand back a smaller version of the same cup. Strength you can fix. Ratio you cannot. If they want it strong, give more leaf and the same time, never the same leaf and more time.",
    facts:[["Reads as","Harsh and dull at once. It grips the sides of the tongue and dries the back of the teeth, the flavour behind that grip has gone flat and murky, and the pot has darkened. The guest calls it too strong and asks for more milk, or sends it back saying it tastes stewed or tastes of the pot, and either way they are describing the same pot. Two tells you should not take as proof. Cloudiness is not it, because any strong tea clouds as it cools and that is cream forming rather than stewing. Ashtray is not it either, because that is a smoked leaf doing exactly what it was bought to do."], ["Comes from","Leaf left in water, or brewed tea held hot, and usually both together, but they are not the same failure and the cup will tell you which. Leaf left in gives you the grip: the aromatic and the sweeter part of a tea come out early while the astringent polyphenols keep coming long afterwards, so a pot left sitting on its leaf gets stronger and disproportionately more drying at the same time, and the ratio of grip to flavour is fixed the moment you leave it there. Holding the finished tea hot does the other half, driving off the aromatics and oxidising what remains, which leaves you dull, heavy and dark rather than more drying. Worth knowing that astringency is a feel and not a taste, a polyphenol binding the proteins in your saliva rather than landing anywhere on the tongue, because that is what decides which remedies can work on it."], ["Correct in","Correct wherever something has been built to meet it. Masala chai is simmered hard because milk, sugar and spice are waiting for it, a punishing pot is the entire point of Hong Kong style milk tea and of Thai iced tea, and a concentrate brewed to be cut at the cup is an old and sound habit. Tea for a cocktail should be brewed strong on purpose, because ice, citrus and syrup are about to dilute it. What none of those do is hold a plain pot of black tea hot for an hour, which is the half of this fault that is correct nowhere."], ["Your move","Get the leaf out of the water on time, including out of the guest's cup, so bring a small dish for the bag or strain the pot at the table rather than leaving it to sit and keep working. Do not hold a plain pot hot: brew to the order, or brew to a schedule and chill it down, and hold only what was built to be held, which is a spiced and sweetened concentrate with milk waiting for it. If a pot has already stewed, dump it. And take the guest's instinct more seriously than your own here, because milk genuinely does cut the grip, since casein binds the polyphenols that were binding their saliva, while hot water from the tap only makes the same cup smaller."]] },
];
